WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.368 --> 00:00:02.869 align:start size:97% position:3%
- The Democrats are definitely
not as screwy as we are,

00:00:02.869 --> 00:00:04.337
and I think they're not
screwy at all actually

00:00:04.337 --> 00:00:05.605
in the Democratic Party.

00:00:05.605 --> 00:00:08.174
They raise money in a
very disciplined way.

00:00:08.174 --> 00:00:10.810 align:start size:88% position:13%
And you know, they do
have their little tiffs,

00:00:10.810 --> 00:00:14.414
personality tiffs between
the squad and Nancy Pelosi.

00:00:14.414 --> 00:00:16.383 align:start size:94% position:6%
But overall, I think
they're very disciplined.

00:00:16.383 --> 00:00:19.386
And now, for the first couple
of years I was at the RNC,

00:00:19.386 --> 00:00:20.920
we would be cheering, "Rah rah,"

00:00:20.920 --> 00:00:24.624 align:start size:97% position:3%
because we raised more money
into the RNC than the DNC

00:00:24.624 --> 00:00:26.593
raises into the DNC.

00:00:26.593 --> 00:00:28.094
And they're quietly laughing,

00:00:28.094 --> 00:00:31.164 align:start size:97% position:3%
because they raised 10 times
as much money as we do,

00:00:31.164 --> 00:00:34.667
but they raised it into
the NAACP, and the ACLU,

00:00:34.667 --> 00:00:36.636 align:start size:88% position:13%
and the Michigan Welfare
Rights Association,

00:00:36.636 --> 00:00:38.938
and La Raza, and
La Familia Vota.

00:00:38.938 --> 00:00:40.440
Those five organizations

00:00:40.440 --> 00:00:42.575
are organizations that I'm
up against in court right now

00:00:42.575 --> 00:00:45.645 align:start size:88% position:13%
for election litigation,
it is not the DNC.

00:00:45.645 --> 00:00:47.080 align:start size:84% position:16%
They have dark
money organizations

00:00:47.080 --> 00:00:48.815
that influence our elections,

00:00:48.815 --> 00:00:52.285
and help pick right
wing candidates in
moderate districts.

00:00:52.285 --> 00:00:54.888
And so that we just
like, we are cooked.

00:00:54.888 --> 00:00:56.489 align:start size:84% position:16%
We're thinking,
"Great, we selected

00:00:56.489 --> 00:00:58.825 align:start size:88% position:13%
this great conservative
candidate."

00:00:58.825 --> 00:01:01.394
That candidate can't
win in that state,

00:01:01.394 --> 00:01:04.297
and so we're just like not
even talking about that.

00:01:04.297 --> 00:01:08.435
So their monetary ecosystem
is far superior to ours,

00:01:08.435 --> 00:01:09.669
and they're very disciplined,

00:01:09.669 --> 00:01:11.538
and we are not, and
we need to get smart.

00:01:11.538 --> 00:01:14.274
(dramatic music)

00:01:18.445 --> 00:01:19.913 align:start size:84% position:16%
- I'm Dave Rubin,
and joining me today

00:01:19.913 --> 00:01:22.248
is the founder of
Dhillon Law Group,

00:01:22.248 --> 00:01:25.752
an absolute free speech warrior.

00:01:25.752 --> 00:01:29.656
I'm talking first team
all free speech warrior,

00:01:29.656 --> 00:01:31.057
and now candidate

00:01:31.057 --> 00:01:33.460 align:start size:94% position:6%
for the Republican
National Committee chair,

00:01:33.460 --> 00:01:34.828
Harmeet Dhillon, Harmeet,

00:01:34.828 --> 00:01:36.429 align:start size:84% position:16%
welcome back to
"The Rubin Report."

00:01:36.429 --> 00:01:37.964
- Thanks for having me.

00:01:37.964 --> 00:01:39.466
- I'm glad to have you, Harmeet,

00:01:39.466 --> 00:01:42.902
and we haven't spoken in
a while, at least on air,

00:01:42.902 --> 00:01:45.071
and I thought you would
be the perfect guest

00:01:45.071 --> 00:01:47.874
to be our last guest of 2022,

00:01:47.874 --> 00:01:49.342
because one of the main messages

00:01:49.342 --> 00:01:51.111 align:start size:84% position:16%
that I'm always
pushing on this show

00:01:51.111 --> 00:01:54.347
is that good people
have to get involved.

00:01:54.347 --> 00:01:56.349 align:start size:81% position:19%
And just in the
last couple weeks,

00:01:56.349 --> 00:01:58.918 align:start size:84% position:16%
you have thrown your
hat in the ring,

00:01:58.918 --> 00:02:00.420
and you're getting involved.

00:02:00.420 --> 00:02:03.790
So my first question, I
suppose is, are you nuts?

00:02:03.790 --> 00:02:06.292 align:start size:97% position:3%
- (chuckling) Yeah, this
question has been asked of me

00:02:06.292 --> 00:02:08.128
by several people who love me.

00:02:08.128 --> 00:02:10.797
And you know,
(Dave laughing)

00:02:10.797 --> 00:02:13.500 align:start size:94% position:6%
there come a few, I'm sure
Dave, you've had them,

00:02:13.500 --> 00:02:16.436
where you were like, "Hey, let
me go start this new thing,"

00:02:16.436 --> 00:02:19.072 align:start size:84% position:16%
or, "let me leave this
cozy thing I have,

00:02:19.072 --> 00:02:20.874
and follow my dream."

00:02:20.874 --> 00:02:22.509
I had that when I,

00:02:22.509 --> 00:02:24.010
I've had that a few
times in my life,

00:02:24.010 --> 00:02:26.079 align:start size:81% position:19%
and I kind of felt
struck by that.

00:02:26.079 --> 00:02:27.881 align:start size:94% position:6%
So I, you know, discussed
it with my husband,

00:02:27.881 --> 00:02:31.484
we prayed over it, and
you know, we decided yes,

00:02:31.484 --> 00:02:32.719
you feel in your heart,

00:02:32.719 --> 00:02:34.154
you have to stand
up and challenge

00:02:34.154 --> 00:02:35.622
what's going on at the RNC,

00:02:35.622 --> 00:02:40.460
because you cannot stand by and
just nod and have, you know,

00:02:41.194 --> 00:02:42.695
another shrimp cocktail,

00:02:42.695 --> 00:02:45.632
and pretend like everything
is going well, when it's not.

00:02:45.632 --> 00:02:48.067
And so that's really, I
really felt compelled.

00:02:48.067 --> 00:02:49.335
I've kind of felt that way

00:02:49.335 --> 00:02:50.603
for the last couple
of years, in fact.

00:02:50.603 --> 00:02:54.474
But, you know, after the
2020 election debacle,

00:02:54.474 --> 00:02:58.011 align:start size:88% position:13%
President Trump endorsed
Ronna, Ronna McDaniel

00:02:58.011 --> 00:02:59.379
for a third term.

00:02:59.379 --> 00:03:01.681
And, you know, that
itself was something

00:03:01.681 --> 00:03:03.716 align:start size:84% position:16%
that a lot of folks
thought, well,

00:03:03.716 --> 00:03:05.385
maybe it was time for
new leadership then.

00:03:05.385 --> 00:03:08.454
Not that it was her fault,
but when you are in charge,

00:03:08.454 --> 00:03:11.324
and perhaps you don't, you
know, quite pivot and respond

00:03:11.324 --> 00:03:15.361
to changing election laws and
other issues, and messaging,

00:03:15.361 --> 00:03:17.397
maybe it's time for
a fresh approach.

00:03:17.397 --> 00:03:19.032
Well, you know, we
didn't have that.

00:03:19.032 --> 00:03:21.634 align:start size:84% position:16%
And so she did promise
in her last run

00:03:21.634 --> 00:03:23.703 align:start size:94% position:6%
that she was going to have
a third and final term.

00:03:23.703 --> 00:03:25.505
And then suddenly, surprisingly,

00:03:25.505 --> 00:03:27.273
she said she's gonna
run for a fourth term.

00:03:27.273 --> 00:03:30.710
And so I said, "Wait a
minute, we have not won,

00:03:30.710 --> 00:03:32.745 align:start size:84% position:16%
in what I call the
definition of winning,

00:03:32.745 --> 00:03:34.814
in three successive
election cycles."

00:03:34.814 --> 00:03:38.351 align:start size:97% position:3%
I mean, here in California,
2018 was a wipe out.

00:03:38.351 --> 00:03:41.554
We had a, it was
like we lost half.

00:03:41.554 --> 00:03:46.559
2020, we lost the White
House, we lost the House.

00:03:47.427 --> 00:03:48.628
We barely clung to the Senate.

00:03:48.628 --> 00:03:51.598 align:start size:84% position:16%
2022, we're barely
clinging to the House,

00:03:51.598 --> 00:03:53.733
such that there is
a leadership race

00:03:53.733 --> 00:03:56.636
that has conservative
members of Congress

00:03:56.636 --> 00:03:59.472 align:start size:88% position:13%
calling each other names
on Twitter, that's,

00:03:59.472 --> 00:04:02.208
and airing their dirty
laundry out in public.

00:04:02.208 --> 00:04:03.876 align:start size:84% position:16%
And we're saying,
"Yeah, let's sign up

00:04:03.876 --> 00:04:05.178
for another two years of this,

00:04:05.178 --> 00:04:06.679
and another election cycle."

00:04:06.679 --> 00:04:09.382
America can't afford
for us to continue

00:04:09.382 --> 00:04:10.817
to be behind the eight ball,

00:04:10.817 --> 00:04:12.652
and playing catch
up with Democrats.

00:04:12.652 --> 00:04:13.886
We just can't.

00:04:13.886 --> 00:04:16.489
And so if nothing changes,

00:04:16.489 --> 00:04:18.091 align:start size:84% position:16%
we are gonna expect
the same thing.

00:04:18.091 --> 00:04:19.759
And I, for one, don't
wanna be sitting here

00:04:19.759 --> 00:04:21.261
twiddling my thumbs while,

00:04:21.261 --> 00:04:23.229
we're 10, 20 years
behind the Democrats.

00:04:23.229 --> 00:04:25.465
And we don't have to
be, because you know,

00:04:25.465 --> 00:04:28.601
Dave, you know, there's so
much energy in our movement,

00:04:28.601 --> 00:04:31.804 align:start size:97% position:3%
there's so many smart,
young and middle-aged people

00:04:31.804 --> 00:04:36.142
outside D.C., people who
are social media savants,

00:04:36.142 --> 00:04:38.077 align:start size:84% position:16%
people who are fluent
with the culture,

00:04:38.077 --> 00:04:40.046
people who get that
the base of our party

00:04:40.046 --> 00:04:43.850 align:start size:88% position:13%
has shifted from NeoCon
cold war warriors,

00:04:43.850 --> 00:04:45.084 align:start size:84% position:16%
and Chamber of
Commerce messaging,

00:04:45.084 --> 00:04:46.719
and tax cuts for the rich,

00:04:46.719 --> 00:04:49.822 align:start size:84% position:16%
to blue collar workers
and young people

00:04:49.822 --> 00:04:51.724
who are energized
about the future,

00:04:51.724 --> 00:04:54.227
and wanna have a
home and a family.

00:04:54.227 --> 00:04:57.063
We are out of touch, and I
don't think we need to be.

00:04:57.063 --> 00:04:59.165
So I'm here to bring
us back into touch,

00:04:59.165 --> 00:05:02.201 align:start size:84% position:16%
and have that attempt
to reform the RNC

00:05:02.201 --> 00:05:04.270
into fitting what the
base of the party is.

00:05:04.270 --> 00:05:06.306
- Okay, so I wanna
unpack all that,

00:05:06.306 --> 00:05:08.374
including some of the
Republican in-fighting,

00:05:08.374 --> 00:05:09.876 align:start size:88% position:13%
and everything else that
you touched on there.

00:05:09.876 --> 00:05:12.779 align:start size:97% position:3%
But before we do all of that,
for anyone in my audience

00:05:12.779 --> 00:05:14.213
that hasn't seen you
on the show before,

00:05:14.213 --> 00:05:15.581
or isn't that familiar,

00:05:15.581 --> 00:05:17.617
could we do a little
Harmeet 101 real quick?

00:05:17.617 --> 00:05:20.787
Why did I describe you
as a free speech warrior?

00:05:21.954 --> 00:05:24.657
- Well, I've been doing
free speech litigation

00:05:24.657 --> 00:05:27.560 align:start size:84% position:16%
literally since
before I was a lawyer.

00:05:27.560 --> 00:05:29.095
I went to Dartmouth at age 16.

00:05:29.095 --> 00:05:30.663 align:start size:81% position:19%
I grew up in rural
North Carolina.

00:05:30.663 --> 00:05:32.632 align:start size:94% position:6%
And at Dartmouth, I
became the editor-in-chief

00:05:32.632 --> 00:05:35.101 align:start size:88% position:13%
of the famous "Dartmouth
Review" newspaper

00:05:35.101 --> 00:05:38.004
that Dinesh D'Souza, Laura
Ingraham, and many, you know,

00:05:38.004 --> 00:05:40.540
now famous people on the
right have been part of.

00:05:40.540 --> 00:05:43.876
And during that time,
three of my colleagues

00:05:43.876 --> 00:05:45.445 align:start size:84% position:16%
were actually kicked
out of Dartmouth

00:05:45.445 --> 00:05:47.680 align:start size:88% position:13%
for something they
wrote about a professor.

00:05:47.680 --> 00:05:50.683 align:start size:97% position:3%
And so back in the day, the
American Civil Liberties Union

00:05:50.683 --> 00:05:52.185
used to actually care
about free speech,

00:05:52.185 --> 00:05:56.189 align:start size:97% position:3%
and they sent us a lawyer
who helped us sue Dartmouth.

00:05:56.189 --> 00:05:58.791
So at the age of 19,
I was on "60 Minutes"

00:05:58.791 --> 00:06:02.362 align:start size:97% position:3%
talking about free speech
issues on American campuses.

00:06:02.362 --> 00:06:05.365 align:start size:81% position:19%
We won, my friends
got reinstated,

00:06:05.365 --> 00:06:08.368 align:start size:97% position:3%
set me off of my pre-med
course into a career in law.

00:06:08.368 --> 00:06:11.003 align:start size:94% position:6%
And so ever since I
graduated from law school,

00:06:11.003 --> 00:06:12.939
now almost 30 years ago,

00:06:12.939 --> 00:06:15.808
I've been working on
issues of liberty.

00:06:15.808 --> 00:06:18.077
And since I started
my own law firm,

00:06:18.077 --> 00:06:19.946
we have focused a lot
on free speech issues

00:06:19.946 --> 00:06:21.214
in virtually every context,

00:06:21.214 --> 00:06:23.082 align:start size:81% position:19%
but we're well
known for our work

00:06:23.082 --> 00:06:26.719
in social media censorship.

00:06:26.719 --> 00:06:29.822 align:start size:94% position:6%
I've sued Twitter a couple
of times, three times.

00:06:29.822 --> 00:06:32.191
We're, you know,
just representing

00:06:32.191 --> 00:06:34.127 align:start size:84% position:16%
half of conservative
social media.

00:06:34.127 --> 00:06:35.561
You know, we represent reporters

00:06:35.561 --> 00:06:37.363
who are sued by politicians.

00:06:37.363 --> 00:06:40.533 align:start size:94% position:6%
We represented "The
Daily Wire" in litigation

00:06:40.533 --> 00:06:42.869 align:start size:84% position:16%
in the United States
Supreme Court.

00:06:42.869 --> 00:06:45.071
And, you know, Project Veritas,

00:06:45.071 --> 00:06:47.173 align:start size:84% position:16%
we've stood up for
their rights in court.

00:06:47.173 --> 00:06:48.875
And so it's a real honor,

00:06:48.875 --> 00:06:50.309
because it's really
the epitome for me.

00:06:50.309 --> 00:06:52.812 align:start size:84% position:16%
The First Amendment
is first for a reason,

00:06:52.812 --> 00:06:54.847
and it's something so special,

00:06:54.847 --> 00:06:56.015 align:start size:81% position:19%
compared to the
rest of the world,

00:06:56.015 --> 00:06:57.550
even the English speaking world.

00:06:57.550 --> 00:07:00.052
They don't have a First
Amendment, we have that.

00:07:00.052 --> 00:07:02.822 align:start size:84% position:16%
And so it's a passion
to do that work,

00:07:02.822 --> 00:07:04.290
and probably 1/3 of the lawyers

00:07:04.290 --> 00:07:07.627
at my 22-lawyer firm do that
work, and it's really exciting.

00:07:07.627 --> 00:07:10.129 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Yeah, and I wanna give
you a little credit publicly

00:07:10.129 --> 00:07:11.264
that I've given you privately,

00:07:11.264 --> 00:07:12.799
which is that over the years,

00:07:12.799 --> 00:07:15.968
when I've had a lot of crazy
hit pieces written about me,

00:07:15.968 --> 00:07:17.603
I've called you a couple times,

00:07:17.603 --> 00:07:19.772 align:start size:94% position:6%
particularly the one with
"The New York Times,"

00:07:19.772 --> 00:07:21.240
when they basically
on the cover said

00:07:21.240 --> 00:07:22.708
I'm the head of the alt-right.

00:07:22.708 --> 00:07:25.278 align:start size:97% position:3%
And I was like, you know, "Can
I sue them, and whatever?"

00:07:25.278 --> 00:07:27.046
And you calmly explained to me

00:07:27.046 --> 00:07:28.915
that unfortunately in
the world we live in,

00:07:28.915 --> 00:07:32.151 align:start size:97% position:3%
even with our libel and
slander laws, like it's tough,

00:07:32.151 --> 00:07:34.554 align:start size:84% position:16%
it's tough, and it'll
probably cost you

00:07:34.554 --> 00:07:36.923
way more than it'll cost
this giant corporation.

00:07:36.923 --> 00:07:39.525
So you've talked me off
the ledge once or twice,

00:07:39.525 --> 00:07:40.993
so I owe you for that.

00:07:42.128 --> 00:07:44.797 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Yeah, absolutely,
and look, to skip away-

00:07:44.797 --> 00:07:47.099
- Now I don't care, for the
record, they keep doing it,

00:07:47.099 --> 00:07:48.601
and it's like, now it's
kind of funny to me,

00:07:48.601 --> 00:07:49.602 align:start size:84% position:16%
but years ago-
- Now, you know what?

00:07:49.602 --> 00:07:51.070
- Yeah?

00:07:51.070 --> 00:07:52.438 align:start size:94% position:6%
- You're bigger than them,
and so what, you know.

00:07:52.438 --> 00:07:54.273
So that, you know,
like, believe me,

00:07:54.273 --> 00:07:57.076 align:start size:97% position:3%
in fact the RNC has been
peddling hit pieces about me,

00:07:57.076 --> 00:07:58.711
and it's very upsetting.

00:07:58.711 --> 00:08:01.013 align:start size:94% position:6%
But luckily I'm
surrounded by calm people,

00:08:01.013 --> 00:08:04.183
who are like, "The truth
is there, you'll prevail."

00:08:04.183 --> 00:08:05.985
And so it's all good.

00:08:05.985 --> 00:08:07.987
But, you know,

00:08:09.655 --> 00:08:12.558
to leave a job that I'm
very passionate about,

00:08:12.558 --> 00:08:14.327 align:start size:84% position:16%
two jobs, I also
started a non-profit,

00:08:14.327 --> 00:08:16.062
it's people who can't
afford or, you know,

00:08:16.062 --> 00:08:20.233
for other reasons, they need
pro bono legal help for free,

00:08:20.233 --> 00:08:21.934
and that is a very,
you know, active

00:08:21.934 --> 00:08:23.669 align:start size:84% position:16%
nonprofit center for
American liberty.

00:08:23.669 --> 00:08:25.404 align:start size:84% position:16%
We represent children
who are targeted

00:08:25.404 --> 00:08:26.939
by transgender groomers.

00:08:26.939 --> 00:08:29.976 align:start size:84% position:16%
We represent people
in free speech issues.

00:08:29.976 --> 00:08:33.012 align:start size:94% position:6%
We represent a host of
other civil rights issues.

00:08:33.012 --> 00:08:35.481
And to leave that
in my law firm,

00:08:35.481 --> 00:08:37.650 align:start size:84% position:16%
which does be
favorite part of law,

00:08:37.650 --> 00:08:40.219 align:start size:97% position:3%
that most lawyers would kill
to be able to do all day,

00:08:40.219 --> 00:08:42.889 align:start size:97% position:3%
and get paid for it, that's
how passionate I feel

00:08:42.889 --> 00:08:44.357
about this RNC thing.

00:08:44.357 --> 00:08:47.126 align:start size:94% position:6%
I mean, I love my job
right now, I love my jobs,

00:08:47.126 --> 00:08:49.829 align:start size:81% position:19%
but I love America
more than that.

00:08:49.829 --> 00:08:51.597
And I think that we're
just driving this thing

00:08:51.597 --> 00:08:53.466
off a cliff here, and
we can't afford it.

00:08:53.466 --> 00:08:55.902
So nobody else stepped
up, so I stepped up.

00:08:55.902 --> 00:08:57.336
I mean, Lee Zeldin
talked about it,

00:08:57.336 --> 00:08:59.071 align:start size:97% position:3%
but ultimately didn't wanna
take on the challenge,

00:08:59.071 --> 00:09:02.108 align:start size:94% position:6%
'cause it is a challenge,
and I stepped up, so.

00:09:02.108 --> 00:09:04.744
I'm gonna see it through.

00:09:04.744 --> 00:09:06.913
- All right, so before we get
into all of that nitty gritty

00:09:06.913 --> 00:09:08.681 align:start size:97% position:3%
that you mentioned earlier,
just a little bit more

00:09:08.681 --> 00:09:10.216 align:start size:84% position:16%
on the big tech stuff
and free speech,

00:09:10.216 --> 00:09:13.152 align:start size:97% position:3%
'cause obviously we're seeing
all of the Twitter files

00:09:13.152 --> 00:09:14.687
dumping right now.

00:09:14.687 --> 00:09:16.355
At the same time, we're also
seeing the mainstream media,

00:09:16.355 --> 00:09:18.090 align:start size:97% position:3%
or the corporate press,
whatever you wanna call them,

00:09:18.090 --> 00:09:19.992 align:start size:84% position:16%
we're seeing them
completely ignore it.

00:09:19.992 --> 00:09:22.495
So our divergence
between the people

00:09:22.495 --> 00:09:23.763
that believe one thing,

00:09:23.763 --> 00:09:24.997 align:start size:84% position:16%
and the people that
believe another thing,

00:09:24.997 --> 00:09:28.000
is just getting wider
and wider and wider.

00:09:28.000 --> 00:09:31.704
Do you think anything
will actually come
out of these leaks?

00:09:31.704 --> 00:09:33.272
I mean, beyond the fact that
a certain amount of people

00:09:33.272 --> 00:09:36.576 align:start position:0%
will wake up, do you think that
there will be legal action,

00:09:36.576 --> 00:09:38.311
either against the
U.S. government,

00:09:38.311 --> 00:09:40.613
or further action
against Twitter?

00:09:40.613 --> 00:09:43.716 align:start size:97% position:3%
And does that then push
things down so that, you know,

00:09:43.716 --> 00:09:45.217
down the road, so that YouTube,

00:09:45.217 --> 00:09:47.186 align:start size:94% position:6%
and Google, and Facebook,
and everybody else

00:09:47.186 --> 00:09:49.855
might start getting looked at
in a different way as well?

00:09:49.855 --> 00:09:51.724 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Well, there is action
against the U.S. government,

00:09:51.724 --> 00:09:55.161
Merrick Schmidt, attorney
general and some others

00:09:55.161 --> 00:09:56.529
are filing suit.

00:09:56.529 --> 00:09:58.598 align:start size:81% position:19%
Before the Twitter
files came out,

00:09:58.598 --> 00:10:01.434 align:start size:94% position:6%
my law firm and the Center
for American Liberty

00:10:01.434 --> 00:10:04.537 align:start size:84% position:16%
sued Twitter and a
host of other parties,

00:10:04.537 --> 00:10:08.140
including California Secretary
of State for this very thing.

00:10:08.140 --> 00:10:12.311
And they, it was revealed
after the 2020 election,

00:10:12.311 --> 00:10:15.014
that California, my
government, right,

00:10:15.014 --> 00:10:16.682
which I pay a lot of taxes to,

00:10:16.682 --> 00:10:18.250
was using those tax dollars.

00:10:18.250 --> 00:10:19.986 align:start size:84% position:16%
Enemies, one of the
enemies is our client,

00:10:19.986 --> 00:10:22.555
Rogan O'Handley, AKA DC Draino,

00:10:22.555 --> 00:10:25.224
who was just reinstated
on Twitter last week.

00:10:25.224 --> 00:10:26.592
Our lawsuit is ongoing.

00:10:26.592 --> 00:10:28.728 align:start size:84% position:16%
That kind of
litigation is ongoing,

00:10:28.728 --> 00:10:30.630 align:start size:97% position:3%
but the courts are not
receptive to it at this time.

00:10:30.630 --> 00:10:32.331 align:start size:97% position:3%
You know, we lost that
case in the district court.

00:10:32.331 --> 00:10:33.633
We argued in the Ninth Circuit,

00:10:33.633 --> 00:10:36.202
you win or lose based
on the perspective

00:10:36.202 --> 00:10:38.471
of the judges who are
on the court, frankly.

00:10:38.471 --> 00:10:40.239
So what is needed, in my opinion

00:10:40.239 --> 00:10:42.108
to really reform this situation,

00:10:42.108 --> 00:10:43.943
is for Republicans
to win elections.

00:10:43.943 --> 00:10:45.678
Republicans have
to win elections,

00:10:45.678 --> 00:10:47.179
and it has to be the
right Republicans.

00:10:47.179 --> 00:10:50.916 align:start size:97% position:3%
If you actually had the,
you know, out of touch crew,

00:10:50.916 --> 00:10:53.753 align:start size:97% position:3%
who's in the Republican
Congress and Senate right now,

00:10:53.753 --> 00:10:55.221
those were the same people

00:10:55.221 --> 00:10:56.555 align:start size:94% position:6%
who were in the Republican
Congress and Senate

00:10:56.555 --> 00:10:59.592
when we had the White
House in 2017 and 2018,

00:10:59.592 --> 00:11:01.060
those were the leaders.

00:11:01.060 --> 00:11:04.196
They did nothing to address
social media censorship.

00:11:04.196 --> 00:11:07.500 align:start size:97% position:3%
We need to elect new and
better Republicans who get it.

00:11:07.500 --> 00:11:10.403 align:start position:0%
We need to reform Communications
Decency Act section 230,

00:11:10.403 --> 00:11:13.039 align:start size:84% position:16%
to not allow
basically a free pass

00:11:13.039 --> 00:11:15.408
to all kinds of torts
and contract violations

00:11:15.408 --> 00:11:18.878
that courts are ignoring
under the ages of 230.

00:11:18.878 --> 00:11:20.446 align:start size:84% position:16%
And then we need to
create, in my opinion,

00:11:20.446 --> 00:11:22.214
of social media users.

00:11:22.214 --> 00:11:23.683
- What do you say to
the counterargument

00:11:23.683 --> 00:11:24.950
on the 230 argument?

00:11:24.950 --> 00:11:25.885
We've discussed it
a lot on the show,

00:11:25.885 --> 00:11:27.386
that some people will say,

00:11:27.386 --> 00:11:29.522 align:start size:94% position:6%
"Well, if you take away
some of those protections,

00:11:29.522 --> 00:11:32.358 align:start size:94% position:6%
that then you'll actually
hamper free speech,

00:11:32.358 --> 00:11:33.592
because the platforms will feel

00:11:33.592 --> 00:11:34.927
that they're more responsible,

00:11:34.927 --> 00:11:36.696
and then they'll have
to go after everybody

00:11:36.696 --> 00:11:39.031
to make sure everything's,
you know, clean or okay."

00:11:39.031 --> 00:11:44.036 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Yeah, I'm not advocating
the removal of 230.

00:11:44.804 --> 00:11:46.038
I don't agree with that.

00:11:46.038 --> 00:11:47.440 align:start size:84% position:16%
There's a provision
of it that says

00:11:47.440 --> 00:11:50.509 align:start size:97% position:3%
that social media companies
can remove content

00:11:50.509 --> 00:11:53.179 align:start size:94% position:6%
that is quote unquote,
"Otherwise objectionable."

00:11:53.179 --> 00:11:54.947
So it isn't in an
enumerated list.

00:11:54.947 --> 00:11:56.615 align:start size:88% position:13%
They use that
otherwise objectionable

00:11:56.615 --> 00:11:59.752
to say vaccine information

00:11:59.752 --> 00:12:01.787
that's contrary to
what Fauci says.

00:12:01.787 --> 00:12:04.790
They use that as an excuse
to censor things that they,

00:12:04.790 --> 00:12:08.360 align:start size:97% position:3%
the social media company, want
to suppress or manipulate.

00:12:08.360 --> 00:12:11.163
Or worse, as we're learning
through the Twitter files,

00:12:11.163 --> 00:12:14.266
they let our government do
that without disclosing it.

00:12:14.266 --> 00:12:16.202
No, I do not think
that was the meaning

00:12:16.202 --> 00:12:18.304
of section 230 at all.

00:12:18.304 --> 00:12:19.772
And Congress can
make that clear.

00:12:19.772 --> 00:12:22.608
The law is old, 1996 law,

00:12:22.608 --> 00:12:25.277 align:start size:94% position:6%
passed to actually protect
social media companies.

00:12:25.277 --> 00:12:26.879
It's an out of control monster,

00:12:26.879 --> 00:12:29.415 align:start size:97% position:3%
and one of the reasons that,
Dave, that you say that,

00:12:29.415 --> 00:12:32.151
you know, there's sort of
a lack of responsiveness

00:12:32.151 --> 00:12:34.386 align:start size:84% position:16%
by the mainstream
media on these issues,

00:12:34.386 --> 00:12:37.289
the mainstream media has
actually been crippled

00:12:37.289 --> 00:12:39.558 align:start size:84% position:16%
by dint of the social
media companies

00:12:39.558 --> 00:12:41.260
having rights and privileges

00:12:41.260 --> 00:12:43.129 align:start size:84% position:16%
to say and do
whatever they want.

00:12:43.129 --> 00:12:44.630
Whereas the traditional media

00:12:44.630 --> 00:12:46.232 align:start size:84% position:16%
doesn't have those
rights and privileges.

00:12:46.232 --> 00:12:49.268 align:start size:84% position:16%
In a way, CDA 230
has really weaponized

00:12:49.268 --> 00:12:50.803
the growth of these,

00:12:50.803 --> 00:12:54.840
the unaccountability of these
big social media companies.

00:12:54.840 --> 00:12:57.676 align:start size:97% position:3%
And so I think there are some
tweaks that could be done

00:12:57.676 --> 00:13:00.412 align:start size:84% position:16%
without empowering
the government,
empowering the users.

00:13:00.412 --> 00:13:03.349
There should be a social
media user's bill of rights

00:13:03.349 --> 00:13:05.885
that says that if they
change the terms on you,

00:13:05.885 --> 00:13:07.386
if they ban you or suppress you

00:13:07.386 --> 00:13:09.622
for reasons that they did
not disclose in advance,

00:13:09.622 --> 00:13:11.090
you have a right to sue them.

00:13:11.090 --> 00:13:12.224
We have rights like that,

00:13:12.224 --> 00:13:14.226 align:start size:88% position:13%
with respect to
pharmaceutical products,

00:13:14.226 --> 00:13:16.595
products that blow up,
cars that don't work.

00:13:16.595 --> 00:13:18.297 align:start size:94% position:6%
We have those rights
with commercial products.

00:13:18.297 --> 00:13:20.599
This is a commercial product,
I'm paying to use Twitter,

00:13:20.599 --> 00:13:23.135
I should have some rights
with respect to Twitter.

00:13:23.135 --> 00:13:25.504
- So what would you
say, I've actually,

00:13:25.504 --> 00:13:27.306 align:start size:97% position:3%
I know you do some work
obviously with Ron Coleman,

00:13:27.306 --> 00:13:29.041
who's been on the
show many times,

00:13:29.041 --> 00:13:31.377
and we discussed
this once on air,

00:13:31.377 --> 00:13:33.445
if I was to find
out, for example,

00:13:33.445 --> 00:13:36.949 align:start size:97% position:3%
that the government directly
had contacted Twitter

00:13:36.949 --> 00:13:41.153
to take down the tweet that
I had back in July of 2021,

00:13:41.153 --> 00:13:43.189
saying that vaccine
mandates were coming,

00:13:43.189 --> 00:13:46.525
and vaccines were not
working, as promised,

00:13:46.525 --> 00:13:48.027
et cetera, et cetera,

00:13:48.027 --> 00:13:49.595
if I was to find out,

00:13:49.595 --> 00:13:51.230
through these Twitter files
or whatever it might be,

00:13:51.230 --> 00:13:54.667 align:start size:97% position:3%
that that actually it was
because of a government agent

00:13:54.667 --> 00:13:56.669
telling Twitter to take it down,

00:13:56.669 --> 00:13:59.471
what is the reward for someone,

00:13:59.471 --> 00:14:02.842 align:start position:0%
other than that maybe the
government would acknowledge it,

00:14:02.842 --> 00:14:04.276
can you sue the government?

00:14:04.276 --> 00:14:07.079 align:start size:97% position:3%
Like, can you be like, "Well,
I want 10 million bucks,

00:14:07.079 --> 00:14:08.914 align:start size:94% position:6%
because you guys infringe
on my free speech?"

00:14:08.914 --> 00:14:11.584 align:start size:97% position:3%
Or is it just purely, just,
"Yeah, we screwed up,"

00:14:11.584 --> 00:14:15.321
it's back up, and nobody gets
fired, this is the government?

00:14:15.321 --> 00:14:16.589
- Well, we have been
suing the government.

00:14:16.589 --> 00:14:18.290 align:start size:88% position:13%
The case I just
described is like that.

00:14:18.290 --> 00:14:21.694
And in the Ninth Circuit,
I think we ran into a,

00:14:21.694 --> 00:14:23.896
let's just say we haven't
received the ruling yet,

00:14:23.896 --> 00:14:26.732 align:start position:0%
but it was not a favorable
reception in the Ninth Circuit.

00:14:26.732 --> 00:14:29.134
I pray that in fact,
at the end of the day,

00:14:29.134 --> 00:14:30.836 align:start size:97% position:3%
these Twitter files
actually started to come out

00:14:30.836 --> 00:14:32.338
after the hearing.

00:14:32.338 --> 00:14:33.706
And so maybe, you know,
the clerks and the judges

00:14:33.706 --> 00:14:35.040
will see that wait, whoa,

00:14:35.040 --> 00:14:36.609
this is actually a huge deal.

00:14:36.609 --> 00:14:39.645
This isn't a one-off, this
is actually a whole system

00:14:39.645 --> 00:14:41.680
that's been exposed.

00:14:41.680 --> 00:14:44.250
A case like that, filed
in a different court,

00:14:44.250 --> 00:14:45.618
might have succeeded,

00:14:45.618 --> 00:14:47.386 align:start size:97% position:3%
but actually under
Twitter's terms of service,

00:14:47.386 --> 00:14:49.221
you have to file it
here in California.

00:14:49.221 --> 00:14:52.524 align:start size:84% position:16%
You know, so it's
been been a challenge.

00:14:52.524 --> 00:14:53.792
I'm confident that over time,

00:14:53.792 --> 00:14:55.494
there will be a case
that breaks through,

00:14:55.494 --> 00:14:57.529
that says that this
is a violation of
the First Amendment.

00:14:57.529 --> 00:14:58.964
I do think it is.

00:14:58.964 --> 00:15:00.432 align:start size:94% position:6%
There are some First
Amendment lawyers who say,

00:15:00.432 --> 00:15:01.800
"Oh no, the companies can
do whatever they want,

00:15:01.800 --> 00:15:04.236 align:start position:0%
it was merely a strong
suggestion from the government."

00:15:04.236 --> 00:15:05.404
- (laughing) Right, right.

00:15:05.404 --> 00:15:06.639
- But here in the Twitter files,

00:15:06.639 --> 00:15:08.507
Twitter was actually
being paid by the FBI.

00:15:08.507 --> 00:15:10.776 align:start size:84% position:16%
I mean, I don't know
how you can deny

00:15:10.776 --> 00:15:12.578
that that's government action.

00:15:12.578 --> 00:15:13.913
It's government
action obviously.

00:15:13.913 --> 00:15:15.881
So I'm sorry I'm very
animated about it,

00:15:15.881 --> 00:15:18.083 align:start size:97% position:3%
but I'm shocked that judges
aren't picking this up.

00:15:18.083 --> 00:15:20.419
- Right, well also if the
government was to call me,

00:15:20.419 --> 00:15:22.388
if I got a call from
a government agent,

00:15:22.388 --> 00:15:24.189
saying, "Dave, we're not
telling you to do anything,

00:15:24.189 --> 00:15:26.025 align:start size:88% position:13%
but just FYI, we've
been watching the show,

00:15:26.025 --> 00:15:27.426 align:start size:84% position:16%
and you've been saying
a lot about that,"

00:15:27.426 --> 00:15:31.263 align:start size:97% position:3%
I might take that as a
threat, or some sort of order.

00:15:31.263 --> 00:15:32.765
- Yeah, or-
- That's why

00:15:32.765 --> 00:15:36.302 align:start size:97% position:3%
the gray area here, obviously,
is where the real stuff is.

00:15:36.302 --> 00:15:37.670
- Right, I mean, or, you know,

00:15:37.670 --> 00:15:40.839 align:start size:97% position:3%
nice social media presence,
and show you have,

00:15:40.839 --> 00:15:42.942
shame if something
were to happen to it,

00:15:42.942 --> 00:15:45.377
or you know, maybe
there's an audit.

00:15:45.377 --> 00:15:46.512 align:start size:88% position:13%
You know, like
literally the government

00:15:46.512 --> 00:15:48.180
has so much power over us,

00:15:48.180 --> 00:15:50.382
we're seeing it with
January 6th stuff.

00:15:50.382 --> 00:15:54.353 align:start size:84% position:16%
I mean, they can
crime and harass you.

00:15:54.353 --> 00:15:57.256 align:start size:81% position:19%
And so this is
not trivial to me.

00:15:57.256 --> 00:16:01.060
And by the way, I, as a
First Amendment lawyer,

00:16:01.060 --> 00:16:03.429
I would stand up for the
rights of some leftist

00:16:03.429 --> 00:16:06.165
to be able to say whatever
they want on social media,

00:16:06.165 --> 00:16:09.635 align:start size:97% position:3%
short of threats of violence
that are true threats,

00:16:09.635 --> 00:16:13.339
or other violations of
enumerated parts of 230,

00:16:13.339 --> 00:16:15.507
pornography and
things like that.

00:16:15.507 --> 00:16:19.278 align:start size:84% position:16%
But that does not
seem to be reciprocal.

00:16:19.278 --> 00:16:21.413
The left has entirely abandoned

00:16:21.413 --> 00:16:24.249
the principle of due process,

00:16:24.249 --> 00:16:26.418 align:start size:97% position:3%
equal protection for
speech issues on the right.

00:16:26.418 --> 00:16:29.922
The ACLU no longer
supports free speech.

00:16:29.922 --> 00:16:32.558
They don't support you being
able to talk about gun use.

00:16:32.558 --> 00:16:35.661 align:start size:84% position:16%
I mean, it is a
180, and it is just

00:16:35.661 --> 00:16:38.430 align:start size:81% position:19%
a wholly awoke
woke endeavor now.

00:16:38.430 --> 00:16:43.202 align:start size:84% position:16%
And so we, the right,
have become the
engine of free speech.

00:16:43.202 --> 00:16:45.404
And even on our side,
people don't get it.

00:16:45.404 --> 00:16:47.506 align:start size:94% position:6%
So I'd love to see a whole
revolution on our side

00:16:47.506 --> 00:16:49.341
about these issues.

00:16:49.341 --> 00:16:50.976
- Okay, so speaking
of a revolution,

00:16:50.976 --> 00:16:53.112
now let's talk about
what your plans are

00:16:53.112 --> 00:16:54.613
to do with the Republicans.

00:16:54.613 --> 00:16:56.715
One of the lines that I say
on this show all the time

00:16:56.715 --> 00:16:58.851
is you don't have
to be a Republican,

00:16:58.851 --> 00:17:00.619
but you cannot be a Democrat.

00:17:00.619 --> 00:17:02.488
And I think that's
really resonated

00:17:02.488 --> 00:17:03.689
with an awful lot of people,

00:17:03.689 --> 00:17:05.858
that the Democrats
have lost their mind,

00:17:05.858 --> 00:17:07.826
The Republicans, it's messy,

00:17:07.826 --> 00:17:09.194 align:start size:81% position:19%
they're gonna fail
you sometimes,

00:17:09.194 --> 00:17:12.064 align:start size:97% position:3%
but at least there's
something there to work with.

00:17:12.064 --> 00:17:14.233
So what would your vision be

00:17:14.233 --> 00:17:16.802 align:start size:97% position:3%
of what the Republican
Party would truly stand for?

00:17:16.802 --> 00:17:18.437
You hit on a little
bit of it earlier,

00:17:18.437 --> 00:17:22.374
but what's the real vision that
you wanna sell to the party?

00:17:22.374 --> 00:17:24.176
- Well, first of all, you
should be a Republican

00:17:24.176 --> 00:17:25.344
if you're not a Democrat.

00:17:25.344 --> 00:17:26.945
(Dave laughing)

00:17:26.945 --> 00:17:28.180
You should not, I believe that,

00:17:28.180 --> 00:17:29.548 align:start size:81% position:19%
let me just put
that on the table.

00:17:29.548 --> 00:17:31.717 align:start size:84% position:16%
It is a zero sum game,
they're two parties

00:17:31.717 --> 00:17:33.252
that are relevant
in this country.

00:17:33.252 --> 00:17:34.820 align:start size:88% position:13%
And if you're not
winning, you're losing.

00:17:34.820 --> 00:17:36.922 align:start size:81% position:19%
That's the fact
of politics, okay.

00:17:36.922 --> 00:17:40.659
So with respect to what
I would do differently,

00:17:40.659 --> 00:17:42.661
there, I mean we probably
don't even have time here,

00:17:42.661 --> 00:17:46.098 align:start position:0%
but look, the party has
evolved in a certain direction.

00:17:46.098 --> 00:17:47.766 align:start size:94% position:6%
It's really been the same
two people running it

00:17:47.766 --> 00:17:48.901
for the last 12 years.

00:17:48.901 --> 00:17:50.369
The base of the party

00:17:50.369 --> 00:17:53.639 align:start size:97% position:3%
has changed in a revolutionary
way during those 12 years.

00:17:53.639 --> 00:17:55.140
Some of the same staff

00:17:55.140 --> 00:17:57.476 align:start size:88% position:13%
from the Reince Priebus
era of six years

00:17:57.476 --> 00:18:00.012
are still running the
show at the RNC today.

00:18:00.012 --> 00:18:01.613 align:start size:84% position:16%
And guess what those
staff are doing?

00:18:01.613 --> 00:18:04.450 align:start size:97% position:3%
They're negotiating contracts
that are self-interested.

00:18:04.450 --> 00:18:06.085
Hundreds of millions of dollars

00:18:06.085 --> 00:18:07.753 align:start size:84% position:16%
has flown into the
pockets of consultants

00:18:07.753 --> 00:18:10.622 align:start size:97% position:3%
over the last few years, and
we haven't won elections.

00:18:10.622 --> 00:18:12.524
The Democrats don't
operate that way.

00:18:12.524 --> 00:18:15.160
The Democrats smartened
up after losing elections

00:18:15.160 --> 00:18:16.595
and paying a bunch of
money to consultants

00:18:16.595 --> 00:18:17.529
almost 20 years ago.

00:18:17.529 --> 00:18:18.764
And they said, "No, no, no,

00:18:18.764 --> 00:18:20.232
we're not gonna
do that anymore."

00:18:20.232 --> 00:18:22.267 align:start position:0%
The party will only do the stuff
that only the party can do.

00:18:22.267 --> 00:18:24.570
And the party plays a
very important role.

00:18:24.570 --> 00:18:26.138
They have certain role,

00:18:26.138 --> 00:18:28.307
with respect to sharing
data with state parties,

00:18:28.307 --> 00:18:31.877
with respect to fundraising,
with respect to, you know,

00:18:31.877 --> 00:18:33.045
they have certain role,

00:18:33.045 --> 00:18:34.446 align:start size:84% position:16%
but a lot of the
stuff the Democrats do

00:18:34.446 --> 00:18:35.848
is outside the party.

00:18:35.848 --> 00:18:37.516 align:start size:94% position:6%
They do it in nonprofits,
pseudo nonprofits,

00:18:37.516 --> 00:18:39.017
PACs and whatever.

00:18:39.017 --> 00:18:41.487
But still the messaging is
led by a party apparatus

00:18:41.487 --> 00:18:45.290
that is disciplined on,
focused on messaging,

00:18:45.290 --> 00:18:46.758
and on their goals.

00:18:46.758 --> 00:18:49.862 align:start position:0%
They do not, you know, have
little disputes with each other

00:18:49.862 --> 00:18:52.865
in the press, and members of
Congress attacking each other.

00:18:52.865 --> 00:18:54.032
That just doesn't happen.

00:18:54.032 --> 00:18:55.200
They have a party discipline,

00:18:55.200 --> 00:18:57.469 align:start size:84% position:16%
and they ruthlessly
collect money

00:18:57.469 --> 00:18:59.371
and dump it into our races.

00:18:59.371 --> 00:19:01.340
And we sit here and say,
as a Republican Party,

00:19:01.340 --> 00:19:02.474
"Uh oh, sorry, our rules say

00:19:02.474 --> 00:19:04.109 align:start size:84% position:16%
we can't do anything
about that."

00:19:04.109 --> 00:19:05.511
I'm not saying that the party

00:19:05.511 --> 00:19:07.246 align:start size:94% position:6%
should be picking
candidates in the primary,

00:19:07.246 --> 00:19:08.647 align:start size:84% position:16%
but what I am saying
is you're foolish

00:19:08.647 --> 00:19:10.682 align:start size:88% position:13%
if you let the Democrats
pick your candidates

00:19:10.682 --> 00:19:11.617
in the primary.

00:19:11.617 --> 00:19:13.452
We have no plan for that.

00:19:13.452 --> 00:19:16.922
The election laws were changed
on a wholesale basis in 2020,

00:19:21.426 --> 00:19:22.928
increment for that.

00:19:22.928 --> 00:19:24.630 align:start size:94% position:6%
Our party was not
raising or spending money

00:19:24.630 --> 00:19:25.864
on election litigation.

00:19:25.864 --> 00:19:27.399
We were like, "Oh, oh,
there's an election?

00:19:27.399 --> 00:19:31.136 align:start size:94% position:6%
Let's have some itinerant,
you know, temp lawyers

00:19:31.136 --> 00:19:34.373
put down their laptops and
work like a lawyer militia,

00:19:34.373 --> 00:19:37.376
and work for free to save
elections for two weeks,

00:19:37.376 --> 00:19:38.844
and then go back to their jobs."

00:19:38.844 --> 00:19:40.445
It doesn't work that way, we
have jobs, we have commitments.

00:19:40.445 --> 00:19:42.147
You can't just like make me
work for free for two weeks.

00:19:42.147 --> 00:19:45.117
So we have to have
been investing like
the Democrats did,

00:19:45.117 --> 00:19:46.885
in lawyers like Mark Elias,

00:19:46.885 --> 00:19:48.887 align:start size:94% position:6%
who is funded to the tune
of whatever he wants,

00:19:48.887 --> 00:19:50.689
and files frivolous
lawsuits sometimes,

00:19:50.689 --> 00:19:53.892
and just spams the courts
with nonsense lawsuits.

00:19:53.892 --> 00:19:56.728
But they get through and they
win if there's no opposition.

00:19:56.728 --> 00:19:59.031
We have not been doing that
until the last two years.

00:19:59.031 --> 00:20:00.732 align:start size:84% position:16%
I will say the
chair listened to me

00:20:00.732 --> 00:20:02.401 align:start size:88% position:13%
jumping up and down
and screaming about it.

00:20:02.401 --> 00:20:05.671 align:start size:97% position:3%
We began to invest in that
issue, we're still way behind.

00:20:05.671 --> 00:20:08.740
Our cost of fundraising,
Dave, is out of control.

00:20:08.740 --> 00:20:10.943
It's like 40%, okay.

00:20:10.943 --> 00:20:13.478 align:start size:88% position:13%
I just came from a
Turning Point USA event,

00:20:13.478 --> 00:20:14.980 align:start size:84% position:16%
Turning Points cost
of fundraising,

00:20:14.980 --> 00:20:17.649
now it's a nonprofit, 7% or so?

00:20:18.817 --> 00:20:20.085 align:start size:84% position:16%
They raise hundreds
of millions of dollars

00:20:20.085 --> 00:20:22.287
over the same time
period that we have.

00:20:22.287 --> 00:20:25.190 align:start size:97% position:3%
Where's that other 30%
going? What's going on there?

00:20:25.190 --> 00:20:26.658
It's going into the
pockets of people

00:20:26.658 --> 00:20:27.993
who are not helping
us win elections.

00:20:27.993 --> 00:20:29.728
I'm very passionate about this.

00:20:29.728 --> 00:20:32.297
Little donors, mom and pops,

00:20:32.297 --> 00:20:36.468 align:start size:97% position:3%
people like my parents and
my, you know, other relatives,

00:20:36.468 --> 00:20:37.936 align:start size:84% position:16%
they say, oh, "Lindsey
Graham needs money,

00:20:37.936 --> 00:20:39.371
you know, let's give him
some money, I like him,

00:20:39.371 --> 00:20:40.639
I saw him on television,"

00:20:40.639 --> 00:20:42.274
and suddenly they're
like signed up forever

00:20:42.274 --> 00:20:43.475
to keep getting emails

00:20:43.475 --> 00:20:44.643 align:start size:84% position:16%
from every Republican
in the universe,

00:20:44.643 --> 00:20:46.211
and they can't stop it.

00:20:46.211 --> 00:20:49.581
The puppy will die, you know,
your mom will be held hostage.

00:20:49.581 --> 00:20:52.084
Like the messages
are so abusive.

00:20:52.084 --> 00:20:53.552 align:start size:84% position:16%
And then big dollar
donors are really sick

00:20:53.552 --> 00:20:55.053
of giving their five
million, 10 million,

00:20:55.053 --> 00:20:57.022
$50 million check, and
we don't win elections.

00:20:57.022 --> 00:20:58.490 align:start size:84% position:16%
I mean that's not what
they're there for.

00:20:58.490 --> 00:21:00.792 align:start size:97% position:3%
So pretty much nobody is
satisfied with the status quo,

00:21:00.792 --> 00:21:03.061
except a significant percentage

00:21:03.061 --> 00:21:04.696 align:start size:97% position:3%
of the members of the
Republican National Committee,

00:21:04.696 --> 00:21:05.998 align:start size:84% position:16%
who have their power,
their privilege,

00:21:05.998 --> 00:21:07.232
their title, their prestige,

00:21:07.232 --> 00:21:09.234
their photographs
of politicians.

00:21:09.234 --> 00:21:11.336 align:start size:94% position:6%
I'm not there for that,
I'm here to win elections,

00:21:11.336 --> 00:21:13.472
so I can go back
and enjoy my life.

00:21:13.472 --> 00:21:17.409 align:start size:88% position:13%
And so we would be
making one of the things

00:21:17.409 --> 00:21:19.878
that really ratcheted up the
ugliness in this race, Dave,

00:21:19.878 --> 00:21:21.113 align:start size:84% position:16%
it'll be very
interesting to know

00:21:21.113 --> 00:21:23.248
that I think on day
three of my campaign,

00:21:23.248 --> 00:21:26.852 align:start size:97% position:3%
I tweeted that I would
initiate a top to bottom audit

00:21:26.852 --> 00:21:30.455 align:start size:97% position:3%
of every consulting and
vendor contract at the RNC.

00:21:30.455 --> 00:21:33.792 align:start size:97% position:3%
And before that, it was a very
gentile exchange of ideas,

00:21:33.792 --> 00:21:35.427 align:start size:84% position:16%
and isn't it cute that
Harmeet's running?

00:21:35.427 --> 00:21:38.096 align:start size:88% position:13%
Suddenly every political
consultant in D.C.

00:21:38.096 --> 00:21:41.533
is out for my blood,
placing hit pieces.

00:21:43.502 --> 00:21:45.003
But it's all gonna come out,

00:21:45.003 --> 00:21:46.705 align:start size:97% position:3%
it's all gonna come out
what's been going on there,

00:21:46.705 --> 00:21:48.373 align:start size:94% position:6%
and then we can talk
about what we do about it,

00:21:48.373 --> 00:21:49.641
because the Democrats don't have

00:21:49.641 --> 00:21:52.878
this rotten, corrupt
system like we do.

00:21:52.878 --> 00:21:54.980
And look, a lot of
people in our RNC,

00:21:54.980 --> 00:21:56.648
most of them are well-meaning,

00:21:56.648 --> 00:21:58.884
but they don't control
what's going on there.

00:21:58.884 --> 00:22:01.086
And so I think, I've
had some conversations

00:22:01.086 --> 00:22:04.389 align:start position:0%
with members who've been
shocked to hear what I've said,

00:22:04.389 --> 00:22:07.926
and I think I changed their
minds, because you know,

00:22:07.926 --> 00:22:10.262 align:start size:81% position:19%
it's a very top
down organization.

00:22:10.262 --> 00:22:12.964 align:start size:97% position:3%
The RNC tells the states,
"If you want us to subsidize

00:22:12.964 --> 00:22:16.668 align:start position:0%
a particular walk app or
method of fundraising, win red,

00:22:16.668 --> 00:22:20.072
you have to use our
system, no choice.

00:22:20.072 --> 00:22:21.239
We don't give you a choice

00:22:21.239 --> 00:22:22.341 align:start size:84% position:16%
as to what works
better in your state."

00:22:22.341 --> 00:22:23.875
That's not Republican.

00:22:23.875 --> 00:22:26.611
The Republican is pushing
power down to the people,

00:22:26.611 --> 00:22:29.247
it's respecting the people,
it's not calling them names.

00:22:29.247 --> 00:22:31.016 align:start size:84% position:16%
Some of my fellow
members of the union

00:22:31.016 --> 00:22:35.253
have called voters low
information bitchers

00:22:35.253 --> 00:22:38.023
for daring to contact them
about this leadership race.

00:22:38.023 --> 00:22:40.125 align:start size:81% position:19%
I love hearing
from those voters,

00:22:40.125 --> 00:22:41.993 align:start size:94% position:6%
I don't call them names
like that, they're voters.

00:22:41.993 --> 00:22:44.596
They may be angry,
sometimes they are,

00:22:44.596 --> 00:22:46.698
but they're angry
for a good reason,

00:22:46.698 --> 00:22:48.367
'cause they're Republicans,
and we don't win elections,

00:22:48.367 --> 00:22:50.402 align:start size:84% position:16%
so of course they're
angry, I'm angry.

00:22:50.402 --> 00:22:54.072 align:start size:94% position:6%
So I think it is with a
relatively simple platform

00:22:54.072 --> 00:22:55.374
that I've been laying out,

00:22:55.374 --> 00:22:58.643
as I'm talking to the
168, 167 other voters,

00:22:58.643 --> 00:23:00.912
but I'm also sharing
that with the public.

00:23:00.912 --> 00:23:04.883 align:start size:84% position:16%
And there is virtually
no support for the
current leadership

00:23:04.883 --> 00:23:08.387
outside the RNC, and
the consultant class

00:23:08.387 --> 00:23:12.324 align:start size:94% position:6%
who feed off of us, and
so that's been heartening.

00:23:12.324 --> 00:23:13.558
And so one way or the other,

00:23:13.558 --> 00:23:15.494 align:start size:81% position:19%
we're gonna see
change at the RNC.

00:23:15.494 --> 00:23:17.028
- Right, I mean I agree
with the premise there.

00:23:17.028 --> 00:23:18.897 align:start size:97% position:3%
It doesn't seem like there's
a lot of support there,

00:23:18.897 --> 00:23:20.465
but for whatever reason,

00:23:20.465 --> 00:23:22.200
and you people can
call it the swamp,

00:23:22.200 --> 00:23:24.970 align:start size:88% position:13%
or the deep state, or
whatever the hell it is,

00:23:24.970 --> 00:23:27.239
it does keep going.

00:23:27.239 --> 00:23:30.776 align:start position:0%
So the outsiders, like you,
always have that uphill battle.

00:23:30.776 --> 00:23:32.244
I see sort of two parts of this.

00:23:32.244 --> 00:23:33.912 align:start size:94% position:6%
You're talking about sort
of the under the hood

00:23:33.912 --> 00:23:36.281 align:start size:94% position:6%
technical stuff, like that
that are the problems,

00:23:36.281 --> 00:23:38.850
in terms of voting and what's
going on with fundraising.

00:23:38.850 --> 00:23:40.619
But I think there's
also the ideal level

00:23:40.619 --> 00:23:42.754 align:start size:94% position:6%
that it seems like there's
a real problem brewing.

00:23:42.754 --> 00:23:46.725
Just in the last couple days,
I heard Mitch McConnell say

00:23:46.725 --> 00:23:51.029 align:start size:94% position:6%
that the number one issue
on Republican's minds

00:23:51.029 --> 00:23:53.698 align:start size:84% position:16%
is defending
Ukraine from Russia.

00:23:53.698 --> 00:23:55.267
And I thought, "Man," you know,

00:23:55.267 --> 00:23:57.035
I'm, for the first
time in my life,

00:23:57.035 --> 00:23:59.304
since I moved to Florida, I
registered as a Republican,

00:23:59.304 --> 00:24:01.006 align:start size:81% position:19%
but I think the
ideas I talk about

00:24:01.006 --> 00:24:03.942
resonate with a lot of
people on both sides,

00:24:03.942 --> 00:24:06.645
it's like, that's
not in my top 20.

00:24:06.645 --> 00:24:07.913 align:start size:88% position:13%
And the way he said it-
- Right.

00:24:07.913 --> 00:24:10.449
- I thought, "Man, I can
now see why Republican,

00:24:10.449 --> 00:24:12.050
you know, the OG Republicans

00:24:12.050 --> 00:24:13.518
have just had it with you guys."

00:24:13.518 --> 00:24:15.420
What do you do about
the messaging part

00:24:15.420 --> 00:24:17.255
that seems very disconnected,

00:24:17.255 --> 00:24:18.723 align:start size:88% position:13%
when he could be talking
about, I don't know,

00:24:18.723 --> 00:24:22.828
the economy, the border,
free speech, et cetera?

00:24:22.828 --> 00:24:24.496 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Well, I can't do anything
about Mitch McConnell.

00:24:24.496 --> 00:24:26.198 align:start size:84% position:16%
If I were in the
United States Senate,

00:24:26.198 --> 00:24:28.300
I'd be voting for
a different leader.

00:24:28.300 --> 00:24:30.235 align:start size:94% position:6%
I can't do anything
about the leadership race

00:24:30.235 --> 00:24:33.205 align:start size:97% position:3%
in the House, where because
we didn't do that well,

00:24:33.205 --> 00:24:34.506
there was no red wave.

00:24:34.506 --> 00:24:36.741 align:start size:88% position:13%
You have this infighting
that's just awful.

00:24:36.741 --> 00:24:39.044 align:start size:97% position:3%
Friends of mine are throwing
stones at each other.

00:24:39.044 --> 00:24:40.812
- Yeah, yeah.
- Unfortunate.

00:24:40.812 --> 00:24:42.547 align:start size:84% position:16%
But I can do something
about the RNC,

00:24:42.547 --> 00:24:44.149
and the RNC does
set the messaging.

00:24:44.149 --> 00:24:45.617
And unfortunately we've
been kind of hostage

00:24:45.617 --> 00:24:47.486
to whatever happened recently,

00:24:47.486 --> 00:24:48.753 align:start size:84% position:16%
whatever the Democrats
said recently,

00:24:48.753 --> 00:24:50.589
we're always like
reacting to that.

00:24:50.589 --> 00:24:53.124 align:start size:94% position:6%
We don't seem to be going
back to our platform,

00:24:53.124 --> 00:24:55.827
or our basic values,

00:24:55.827 --> 00:24:57.729
or frankly even taking a poll,

00:24:57.729 --> 00:25:00.732 align:start position:0%
and then talking about what
Republicans actually care about.

00:25:00.732 --> 00:25:04.102 align:start size:94% position:6%
We spend millions, during
this chair's tenure,

00:25:04.102 --> 00:25:06.137 align:start size:88% position:13%
there have been millions
of dollars spent

00:25:06.137 --> 00:25:10.575
on consultants, on messaging
issues, believe it or not.

00:25:10.575 --> 00:25:12.544
And what you see is
the result of that.

00:25:13.845 --> 00:25:15.380 align:start size:97% position:3%
It's not very good
messaging, I'm sorry to say,

00:25:15.380 --> 00:25:16.581
sorry to offend the people

00:25:16.581 --> 00:25:18.083 align:start size:84% position:16%
who are probably
gonna be very angry,

00:25:18.083 --> 00:25:20.519
and set out more hit pieces
about me because of that,

00:25:20.519 --> 00:25:21.753
but it's true.

00:25:21.753 --> 00:25:23.522
Our base does not
like or appeal,

00:25:23.522 --> 00:25:25.557 align:start size:84% position:16%
the messaging did
not turn out voters.

00:25:25.557 --> 00:25:27.959
The candidates did
not turn out voters,

00:25:27.959 --> 00:25:29.928
and we sat there
like sitting ducks,

00:25:29.928 --> 00:25:31.363
spending a bunch
of money on them.

00:25:31.363 --> 00:25:32.931
And you know, some of
these, like I said,

00:25:32.931 --> 00:25:36.768
back to the issue left
to find our candidates,

00:25:36.768 --> 00:25:38.270
we're letting that happen

00:25:38.270 --> 00:25:40.272
because we're not doing a
good job of picking them

00:25:40.272 --> 00:25:41.239
and defining them.

00:25:41.239 --> 00:25:42.741
There's no party.

00:25:42.741 --> 00:25:44.876
Maybe there needs to be more
of a like cabal situation,

00:25:44.876 --> 00:25:46.511
where you get together
and say, "Look guys,

00:25:46.511 --> 00:25:47.779
we're not gonna win in Georgia,

00:25:47.779 --> 00:25:49.014
and we're not gonna
win in Pennsylvania.

00:25:49.014 --> 00:25:50.682
We're not gonna win in
this state or that state

00:25:50.682 --> 00:25:52.817 align:start size:97% position:3%
if we don't have a candidate
who fits this profile."

00:25:52.817 --> 00:25:54.853 align:start size:84% position:16%
That means our data
has to be good.

00:25:54.853 --> 00:25:57.622
Our messaging has to be good.

00:25:57.622 --> 00:25:59.558
We have to have enough money,

00:25:59.558 --> 00:26:03.495 align:start size:97% position:3%
and we have to be doing
litigation around the clock.

00:26:03.495 --> 00:26:07.832 align:start position:0%
Election integrity is not a
seasonal itinerate worker deal.

00:26:07.832 --> 00:26:10.235 align:start size:84% position:16%
I mean, you know,
the RNC has laid off

00:26:10.235 --> 00:26:12.837 align:start size:97% position:3%
most of its election integrity
staff after the election,

00:26:12.837 --> 00:26:15.907
as if they're like, we picked
the crop, and now we're done.

00:26:15.907 --> 00:26:17.375 align:start size:75% position:25%
(Dave laughing)
No, no, no,

00:26:17.375 --> 00:26:20.045 align:start size:97% position:3%
we need to be doing, we
are so far behind the left,

00:26:20.045 --> 00:26:23.515 align:start size:88% position:13%
we need to be doing this
for a decade or two,

00:26:23.515 --> 00:26:24.983
until we reverse the tide.

00:26:24.983 --> 00:26:27.285 align:start size:97% position:3%
And we need to be spending
10X what we're spending on it.

00:26:27.285 --> 00:26:29.621
- Does it seem sort of
too black-pilled to you

00:26:29.621 --> 00:26:31.723 align:start size:97% position:3%
when people say, "Well
look, you've got California,

00:26:31.723 --> 00:26:33.758
the most populous
state in the union,

00:26:33.758 --> 00:26:35.560 align:start size:81% position:19%
and it's one party
at this point,

00:26:35.560 --> 00:26:37.495 align:start size:84% position:16%
and it's over, and
it's corrupt, we know.

00:26:37.495 --> 00:26:38.997
Just kinda let it be."

00:26:38.997 --> 00:26:42.467
I mean, I tried, I tried, I
could not take it anymore.

00:26:42.467 --> 00:26:43.902
I don't know if you know
this, by the way, Harmeet,

00:26:43.902 --> 00:26:45.637 align:start size:84% position:16%
three days after
the recall election,

00:26:45.637 --> 00:26:47.572 align:start size:94% position:6%
that obviously I was
helping Larry Elder with,

00:26:47.572 --> 00:26:49.074
I was audited by the state,

00:26:49.074 --> 00:26:51.009
coincidentally the same
day that I sold my house,

00:26:51.009 --> 00:26:52.877
or put my house for sale.

00:26:52.877 --> 00:26:54.079
But what do you say to that?

00:26:54.079 --> 00:26:55.313
Like, the people
that are just like,

00:26:55.313 --> 00:26:56.715
"No, we just have to
concede the ground

00:26:56.715 --> 00:26:57.949
of some of these states,

00:26:57.949 --> 00:27:00.852
'cause the machinery
is so entrenched."

00:27:02.020 --> 00:27:05.523
- Well, I can see the
ocean from my home,

00:27:05.523 --> 00:27:07.158
and that's why I
live in California.

00:27:07.158 --> 00:27:08.326
(Dave laughing)

00:27:08.326 --> 00:27:09.628 align:start size:88% position:13%
- And can't do that in-
- We got an ocean

00:27:09.628 --> 00:27:10.662
here in Florida, you know.

00:27:10.662 --> 00:27:12.297 align:start size:88% position:13%
It's a different ocean-
- I know.

00:27:12.297 --> 00:27:13.465
- But it's pretty good.

00:27:13.465 --> 00:27:15.166 align:start size:88% position:13%
- You know, the Pacific
is more beautiful.

00:27:15.166 --> 00:27:18.236
Florida's great too, but I
don't like those giant bugs

00:27:18.236 --> 00:27:19.471
you have over there.

00:27:19.471 --> 00:27:23.008 align:start size:81% position:19%
But seriously,
no, we cannot seat

00:27:23.008 --> 00:27:25.243
any part of America to the left.

00:27:25.243 --> 00:27:28.613
And look, I mean those of
us who didn't get the memo

00:27:28.613 --> 00:27:31.850
were still like fighting
behind enemy lines here,

00:27:31.850 --> 00:27:33.084
like it was World War II.

00:27:33.084 --> 00:27:34.552 align:start size:84% position:16%
I mean, we were told
the war was over.

00:27:34.552 --> 00:27:37.355
Some of us are still
fighting that war here.

00:27:37.355 --> 00:27:40.659 align:start size:88% position:13%
We picked up seats in
the House in California,

00:27:40.659 --> 00:27:42.327
old fashioned work.

00:27:42.327 --> 00:27:45.764 align:start size:94% position:6%
John Duarte, I raised
$100,000 for John Duarte,

00:27:45.764 --> 00:27:48.867 align:start size:88% position:13%
our new member of
Congress in California.

00:27:48.867 --> 00:27:50.535 align:start size:94% position:6%
Hey, you know, he is
from a farming community,

00:27:50.535 --> 00:27:52.370
he's a farming, he's a farmer.

00:27:52.370 --> 00:27:54.205
Go figure, you know,
you picked a candidate

00:27:54.205 --> 00:27:55.540
who's like the district,

00:27:55.540 --> 00:27:57.208 align:start size:97% position:3%
and he's a good communicator
who's authentic.

00:27:57.208 --> 00:27:59.044 align:start size:84% position:16%
He had the money,
'cause people like me,

00:27:59.044 --> 00:28:00.345 align:start size:84% position:16%
and others helped
him raise the money.

00:28:00.345 --> 00:28:03.048 align:start size:84% position:16%
But the RNC did
invest in California

00:28:03.048 --> 00:28:04.849
to help in targeted districts.

00:28:04.849 --> 00:28:08.753 align:start position:0%
And we, the party of California,
pulled that off, okay.

00:28:08.753 --> 00:28:10.722 align:start size:84% position:16%
So I think there
are a lot of factors

00:28:10.722 --> 00:28:12.223
that contributed to that.

00:28:12.223 --> 00:28:14.926 align:start size:94% position:6%
That's good, because we
went from 14 seats in 2016

00:28:14.926 --> 00:28:17.729
to seven seats in 2018.

00:28:17.729 --> 00:28:18.963
My god, that was a wipe out,

00:28:18.963 --> 00:28:20.765 align:start size:81% position:19%
and then we've
slowly built back,

00:28:20.765 --> 00:28:22.467
even as we've lost a
congressional seat.

00:28:22.467 --> 00:28:23.635
So no, it's not hopeless.

00:28:23.635 --> 00:28:27.472 align:start size:84% position:16%
But I mean the recall
was kind of sad.

00:28:27.472 --> 00:28:31.509
Larry Elder was a dynamic
candidate, and all of that.

00:28:31.509 --> 00:28:33.845 align:start size:97% position:3%
Getting your campaign
started at the last minute,

00:28:33.845 --> 00:28:35.714
and never having run
for office before,

00:28:35.714 --> 00:28:38.983 align:start size:97% position:3%
and the first thing you
run for, advocate for that.

00:28:38.983 --> 00:28:41.152 align:start size:97% position:3%
I definitely think that
people should learn the ropes.

00:28:41.152 --> 00:28:42.921 align:start size:94% position:6%
We've been encouraging
people here in California,

00:28:42.921 --> 00:28:45.490
in this election cycle, to step
up and run for school board.

00:28:45.490 --> 00:28:47.726 align:start size:97% position:3%
Are you a mom or a dad, you're
upset with what's going on?

00:28:47.726 --> 00:28:49.928 align:start size:88% position:13%
School board is not very
expensive to run for,

00:28:49.928 --> 00:28:53.164 align:start position:0%
it's easy to campaign for, it's
a small universe of voters,

00:28:53.164 --> 00:28:54.899 align:start size:88% position:13%
and we had some
success in that regard.

00:28:54.899 --> 00:28:56.601 align:start size:84% position:16%
We have to be training
people to do that.

00:28:56.601 --> 00:28:58.136
I've run for office twice.

00:28:58.136 --> 00:29:00.505
Unlike the current chair, I
actually know what it means

00:29:00.505 --> 00:29:01.673
to actually like,
stand out there,

00:29:01.673 --> 00:29:03.374 align:start size:84% position:16%
put your money
where your mouth is,

00:29:03.374 --> 00:29:06.444
and actually stand at the
BART, the subway station,

00:29:06.444 --> 00:29:09.314
hand out cards, talk to
hostile groups of people,

00:29:09.314 --> 00:29:11.149
raise money from strangers.

00:29:11.149 --> 00:29:13.451
It's a skillset that
you cannot just get

00:29:13.451 --> 00:29:14.586
'cause you have great ideas.

00:29:14.586 --> 00:29:16.554
It is a craft, everyone.

00:29:16.554 --> 00:29:18.823
So I think it, I think
that we need to have,

00:29:18.823 --> 00:29:20.825 align:start size:81% position:19%
and we had another
good candidate,

00:29:20.825 --> 00:29:22.894
the mayor of San Diego,

00:29:22.894 --> 00:29:25.630 align:start size:97% position:3%
and you know, once Larry came
in, who was very popular,

00:29:25.630 --> 00:29:27.298
that was just, that
race was just over.

00:29:27.298 --> 00:29:30.401 align:start position:0%
But I don't think Larry had
built the team necessary to win.

00:29:30.401 --> 00:29:35.173 align:start size:97% position:3%
And so George Gascon recall,
okay, in Los Angeles,

00:29:35.173 --> 00:29:37.408
that should have succeeded.

00:29:37.408 --> 00:29:40.078
I believe it bungled
because of consultants.

00:29:40.078 --> 00:29:43.181 align:start size:88% position:13%
Consultants, who did
the signature gathering,

00:29:43.181 --> 00:29:44.983
did not do their jobs correctly,

00:29:44.983 --> 00:29:46.785 align:start size:94% position:6%
and there was no
transparency around that,

00:29:46.785 --> 00:29:49.120 align:start size:88% position:13%
and now it's degenerated
into investigations

00:29:49.120 --> 00:29:50.688
and all of that, what a tragedy.

00:29:50.688 --> 00:29:54.859 align:start size:94% position:6%
But here in San Francisco,
in a liberal city,

00:29:54.859 --> 00:29:57.128
we got rid of the school board,

00:29:57.128 --> 00:29:59.364
and we got rid of our
district attorney.

00:29:59.364 --> 00:30:01.432
So there is a point at
which even liberals,

00:30:01.432 --> 00:30:05.637
even even $12 avocado
toast-eating liberals

00:30:05.637 --> 00:30:08.139
will say, "No more,
I don't want feces

00:30:08.139 --> 00:30:11.609 align:start size:84% position:16%
in front of my hip
loft condo, I'm done."

00:30:11.609 --> 00:30:14.445
So yes, I do think that
that they're persuadable.

00:30:14.445 --> 00:30:15.814 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Yeah, George Gascon,
who you mentioned,

00:30:15.814 --> 00:30:17.081
just for people that don't know,

00:30:17.081 --> 00:30:18.683
he's the former DA
of San Francisco,

00:30:18.683 --> 00:30:21.152
who ruined San Francisco,
then moved down to LA.

00:30:21.152 --> 00:30:22.654
Bernie backed, of course,

00:30:22.654 --> 00:30:25.123
basically ruined LA, and then
the recall against him fell.

00:30:25.123 --> 00:30:26.357
But as you referenced,

00:30:26.357 --> 00:30:28.827 align:start size:84% position:16%
the recall of Boudin
in San Francisco,

00:30:28.827 --> 00:30:30.228
that one actually worked.

00:30:30.228 --> 00:30:32.330 align:start size:94% position:6%
Have you seen any
changes in San Francisco?

00:30:32.330 --> 00:30:34.532
Is there any chance that
because they got rid of

00:30:34.532 --> 00:30:36.868
some of these guys,
and have, I suppose,

00:30:36.868 --> 00:30:39.003
slightly less crazed lefties,

00:30:39.003 --> 00:30:41.039
that things are
gonna get better?

00:30:41.039 --> 00:30:43.775 align:start position:0%
- I would call, I would
describe it as a marginal change

00:30:43.775 --> 00:30:47.178
but the rep from the
DA is much better.

00:30:47.178 --> 00:30:48.613
She's talking about
prosecuting crimes,

00:30:48.613 --> 00:30:50.014
she is prosecuting crimes.

00:30:50.014 --> 00:30:51.282
I don't think we're gonna,

00:30:51.282 --> 00:30:53.651
I mean, at the depth
of, earlier this year,

00:30:53.651 --> 00:30:55.119
Chesa Boudin's tenure,

00:30:55.119 --> 00:31:00.091 align:start size:94% position:6%
elderly Asians were
regularly getting stabbed,

00:31:01.492 --> 00:31:04.796 align:start size:97% position:3%
assaulted, knocked over,
killed, with no prosecution.

00:31:04.796 --> 00:31:05.964
That's not happening anymore.

00:31:05.964 --> 00:31:07.398 align:start size:84% position:16%
I haven't read a
single story like that

00:31:07.398 --> 00:31:09.067 align:start size:97% position:3%
