are made to me.

00:31:23.615 --> 00:31:25.416
I really want there to
be a district attorney

00:31:25.416 --> 00:31:26.951
who cares about that stuff.

00:31:26.951 --> 00:31:28.987
So I think she's an improvement.

00:31:28.987 --> 00:31:30.688 align:start size:84% position:16%
Could we, would I
one day like to vote

00:31:30.688 --> 00:31:32.190
for a Republican for that race?

00:31:32.190 --> 00:31:35.326
Absolutely, but you know,
I'll take a better Democrat

00:31:35.326 --> 00:31:36.961
where that's the only choice.

00:31:36.961 --> 00:31:39.530
So I think that's
a good development.

00:31:39.530 --> 00:31:42.600
Now we have to keep it up.

00:31:42.600 --> 00:31:45.503
But San Francisco
has been absolute,

00:31:45.503 --> 00:31:47.038 align:start size:84% position:16%
that's not the point
of your question,

00:31:47.038 --> 00:31:50.875 align:start size:94% position:6%
but this city is so
hollowed out and decimated

00:31:50.875 --> 00:31:54.078
by the combination of COVID
lockdowns, work from home,

00:31:54.078 --> 00:31:57.015 align:start position:0%
Twitter and other big companies
packing up and leaving.

00:31:57.015 --> 00:31:59.717 align:start size:81% position:19%
It is not the same
city as it was.

00:31:59.717 --> 00:32:03.121 align:start size:88% position:13%
And so yes, in a
Detroit-type situation,

00:32:03.121 --> 00:32:05.623 align:start size:97% position:3%
where people are leaving
abandoned houses, and stuff,

00:32:05.623 --> 00:32:09.093 align:start size:97% position:3%
and yes, you do see a
certain level of, you know,

00:32:09.093 --> 00:32:12.597 align:start size:97% position:3%
the most unfortunate of
society wandering the streets.

00:32:12.597 --> 00:32:14.065
And some of them
are mentally ill,

00:32:14.065 --> 00:32:17.502 align:start size:97% position:3%
many of them are using
drugs, and it is dangerous.

00:32:17.502 --> 00:32:18.503
I don't walk to work anymore

00:32:18.503 --> 00:32:19.804
like I used to in San Francisco.

00:32:19.804 --> 00:32:21.973
So how long I'm able
to put up with this

00:32:21.973 --> 00:32:24.309
is an open question
in our household.

00:32:24.309 --> 00:32:26.277
- Yeah, the last time
I was in San Francisco

00:32:26.277 --> 00:32:29.781
was literally the day they
were announcing lockdowns

00:32:29.781 --> 00:32:31.015
for the first time.

00:32:31.015 --> 00:32:32.850 align:start size:84% position:16%
I was there to raise
money for locals,

00:32:32.850 --> 00:32:35.720
on the day that the
market was crashing.

00:32:35.720 --> 00:32:37.622
And I felt being
in San Francisco

00:32:37.622 --> 00:32:39.123
was sort of a metaphor for that.

00:32:39.123 --> 00:32:41.859
But, all right, so let's
move then from that coast,

00:32:41.859 --> 00:32:43.294
to where I am in Florida,

00:32:43.294 --> 00:32:45.964 align:start size:97% position:3%
where now obviously
DeSantis wins by 20 points.

00:32:45.964 --> 00:32:49.033 align:start size:97% position:3%
He's got a, the Republicans
have super majority,

00:32:49.033 --> 00:32:52.870 align:start size:97% position:3%
there's basically
everything is working here.

00:32:52.870 --> 00:32:54.772 align:start size:81% position:19%
Do you view what's
going on here,

00:32:54.772 --> 00:32:57.308
election integrity,
which is working,

00:32:57.308 --> 00:33:00.745 align:start size:94% position:6%
do you view this
experiment here in Florida

00:33:00.745 --> 00:33:03.047
as sort of the blueprint
of what you could export

00:33:03.047 --> 00:33:04.682
throughout the country?

00:33:04.682 --> 00:33:06.951 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Well, I mean I certainly
am very impressed

00:33:06.951 --> 00:33:10.188 align:start position:0%
with what's happened in
Florida, it is truly impressive.

00:33:10.188 --> 00:33:13.958 align:start size:94% position:6%
And even with early
voting, they mastered it.

00:33:13.958 --> 00:33:15.660
You know, the RNC
really has not.

00:33:15.660 --> 00:33:18.363 align:start size:94% position:6%
I saw the chair yesterday
claiming credit for,

00:33:18.363 --> 00:33:20.365
"Oh, we did this, we did that."

00:33:20.365 --> 00:33:25.370 align:start size:97% position:3%
And in fact, you know, it's
really state leadership

00:33:26.104 --> 00:33:27.372
that makes it happen.

00:33:27.372 --> 00:33:28.873
The RNC may provide
financial resources,

00:33:28.873 --> 00:33:31.542 align:start size:97% position:3%
but it hasn't really provided
the training to do that.

00:33:31.542 --> 00:33:34.345
Yeah, I wish I could live
in a utopian, you know,

00:33:34.345 --> 00:33:36.247 align:start size:84% position:16%
Republican paradise,
like Florida,

00:33:36.247 --> 00:33:38.116
with the tax rates
and the, you know,

00:33:38.116 --> 00:33:42.186
the wonderful non
lockdown situation.

00:33:42.186 --> 00:33:44.288 align:start size:84% position:16%
But I wish that was
California. (laughing)

00:33:44.288 --> 00:33:45.656 align:start size:84% position:16%
But yes, I think
it's very impressive,

00:33:45.656 --> 00:33:46.891
and leadership does matter,

00:33:46.891 --> 00:33:51.829
and so Republicans can
efficiently run a state,

00:33:52.997 --> 00:33:54.465
and bring it back, it's
been very impressive.

00:33:55.700 --> 00:33:58.336
- Where do the presidential,

00:33:58.336 --> 00:34:01.105 align:start size:94% position:6%
or potential presidential
candidates of '24,

00:34:01.105 --> 00:34:03.841
what kind of role
do they play in,

00:34:03.841 --> 00:34:05.343
I guess what you're doing now,

00:34:05.343 --> 00:34:07.879
and what you would be doing if
you actually win this thing?

00:34:07.879 --> 00:34:11.582 align:start position:0%
Because obviously there's really
two things happening here.

00:34:11.582 --> 00:34:13.518
There's the Trump
portion of this,

00:34:13.518 --> 00:34:16.320 align:start size:97% position:3%
and then there's this
either maybe DeSantis thing,

00:34:16.320 --> 00:34:18.022
or just something
that is not Trump.

00:34:18.022 --> 00:34:20.591
How does that factor in to
what you're doing right now,

00:34:20.591 --> 00:34:21.793
and then what you would do

00:34:21.793 --> 00:34:24.762
as the potential
head of this thing?

00:34:24.762 --> 00:34:28.232 align:start size:97% position:3%
- So President Trump selected
Ronna Romney McDaniel

00:34:28.232 --> 00:34:30.234
to become the chair in 2016,

00:34:30.234 --> 00:34:32.236 align:start size:88% position:13%
when Reince Priebus
went to the White House

00:34:32.236 --> 00:34:33.671
as his chief of staff.

00:34:33.671 --> 00:34:37.241 align:start size:88% position:13%
Some of the RNC staff
went to the White House,

00:34:37.241 --> 00:34:40.645
and then some of them were
let go, for various reasons,

00:34:40.645 --> 00:34:41.879
and they went back to the RNC.

00:34:41.879 --> 00:34:43.114
So it's basically
been the same folks

00:34:43.114 --> 00:34:44.482
running the show over there.

00:34:44.482 --> 00:34:47.718 align:start size:88% position:13%
President Trump
endorsed Ronna in 2018,

00:34:47.718 --> 00:34:50.455 align:start size:84% position:16%
after we had a
disastrous election,

00:34:50.455 --> 00:34:53.891
and again in 2020 after
he lost the White House.

00:34:53.891 --> 00:34:57.829
So he has not weighed in on
this election cycle as yet.

00:34:57.829 --> 00:34:59.931
Although I saw a report
in the Associated Press

00:34:59.931 --> 00:35:01.499
a couple of days
ago that, you know,

00:35:01.499 --> 00:35:04.068
one of his top advisors
is quietly telling people

00:35:04.068 --> 00:35:06.270
he's supporting the current
leadership for a fourth term.

00:35:06.270 --> 00:35:09.140 align:start size:94% position:6%
So, I mean, I'm one of
President Trump's lawyers,

00:35:09.140 --> 00:35:10.675
and my law firm represents,

00:35:10.675 --> 00:35:12.543
my partner's represent him
in several other matters,

00:35:12.543 --> 00:35:14.045
besides what I've done.

00:35:14.045 --> 00:35:19.050 align:start size:97% position:3%
So I'm not gonna change what
I'm doing based on that.

00:35:20.451 --> 00:35:21.719 align:start size:94% position:6%
This is something that
comes from my heart to do.

00:35:21.719 --> 00:35:23.154
It isn't based on a
particular candidate

00:35:23.154 --> 00:35:24.422
telling me what to do.

00:35:24.422 --> 00:35:25.890 align:start size:84% position:16%
So, and I don't
think it's, you know,

00:35:25.890 --> 00:35:28.559 align:start size:97% position:3%
appropriate for a
candidate for this position

00:35:28.559 --> 00:35:31.429
to seek the endorsement of
a presidential candidate,

00:35:31.429 --> 00:35:33.898
because the RNC is meant to
be neutral on these issues.

00:35:33.898 --> 00:35:35.700 align:start size:84% position:16%
So that is my
commitment to voters,

00:35:35.700 --> 00:35:38.102
'cause we have people on
both sides in the RNC.

00:35:38.102 --> 00:35:39.637
We have a lot of
Trump supporters.

00:35:39.637 --> 00:35:42.707
I was a two-time Trump voter.

00:35:42.707 --> 00:35:43.841
And then we have some people

00:35:43.841 --> 00:35:45.610 align:start size:84% position:16%
who are also two-time
Trump voters,

00:35:45.610 --> 00:35:46.811 align:start size:84% position:16%
who wanna see
something different.

00:35:46.811 --> 00:35:48.813 align:start size:84% position:16%
And so, you know,
the voters in America

00:35:48.813 --> 00:35:51.115
are gonna decide that,
I'm not gonna decide it.

00:35:52.250 --> 00:35:54.552
So I don't think any
of the other candidates

00:35:54.552 --> 00:35:56.154 align:start size:94% position:6%
have weighed in formally,
and that's okay,

00:35:56.154 --> 00:35:58.523
because I think that, you
know, we really need to see

00:35:58.523 --> 00:36:00.324
what Republicans and
the grassroots want.

00:36:00.324 --> 00:36:01.425
This isn't a top-down party,

00:36:01.425 --> 00:36:02.693
in my opinion, it should not be.

00:36:02.693 --> 00:36:04.762
And so I very much
hope that we have

00:36:04.762 --> 00:36:07.965 align:start size:94% position:6%
a grassroots-oriented
outcome to this election.

00:36:07.965 --> 00:36:11.969
- What's the technical part of
how you get elected actually?

00:36:13.571 --> 00:36:14.739 align:start size:94% position:6%
- We have a-
- Like how does the leader

00:36:14.739 --> 00:36:16.207
actually get elected?

00:36:16.207 --> 00:36:17.975 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Yeah, we have a winter
meeting of the RNC

00:36:17.975 --> 00:36:22.947
in Dana Point, California,
and there are 168 voters.

00:36:22.947 --> 00:36:25.716
There're 50 states,
and six territories.

00:36:25.716 --> 00:36:27.218
Each of them has three members,

00:36:27.218 --> 00:36:29.687
a national committee man,
a national committee woman,

00:36:29.687 --> 00:36:31.489
and a chair of the party.

00:36:31.489 --> 00:36:33.424
And so you have to, you
know, you have to lobby

00:36:33.424 --> 00:36:34.926
with each of those.

00:36:34.926 --> 00:36:36.627 align:start size:97% position:3%
Now if you're the chair, you
can cut deals with people

00:36:36.627 --> 00:36:38.262 align:start size:81% position:19%
based on resources
that you have.

00:36:38.262 --> 00:36:40.231
That's just a fact, some
members are very upset

00:36:40.231 --> 00:36:41.933
that I said that at the
beginning of my campaign,

00:36:41.933 --> 00:36:43.501
Hey, guess what?

00:36:43.501 --> 00:36:46.304 align:start size:94% position:6%
Some of them are shocked,
shocked to find out

00:36:46.304 --> 00:36:48.439 align:start size:94% position:6%
that politics is happening
in this establishment.

00:36:48.439 --> 00:36:49.874
Yeah, that's politics.

00:36:49.874 --> 00:36:52.176 align:start size:94% position:6%
Yeah, you scratch my
back, I'll scratch yours.

00:36:52.176 --> 00:36:54.645 align:start size:84% position:16%
You change your vote
from Bob to Sue,

00:36:54.645 --> 00:36:56.647
and I'll make sure you're
the head of that committee.

00:36:56.647 --> 00:36:59.150 align:start size:97% position:3%
That happens at the RNC
since the beginning of time.

00:36:59.150 --> 00:37:00.518 align:start size:84% position:16%
But I don't have
that ability, right,

00:37:00.518 --> 00:37:03.054
'cause I'm not able
to hand out goodies.

00:37:03.054 --> 00:37:05.623
So that's, but I have my ideas.

00:37:05.623 --> 00:37:08.659
I have millions of
Republican voters

00:37:08.659 --> 00:37:11.495
who want to see a
change at the RNC,

00:37:11.495 --> 00:37:14.465
and ultimately the
members of the RNC

00:37:14.465 --> 00:37:18.002 align:start size:84% position:16%
are gonna have to
face reelection, or
election themselves.

00:37:18.002 --> 00:37:21.305
And there's gonna be a question

00:37:21.305 --> 00:37:25.876
as to who did you support in
the 2022, 2023 leadership race?

00:37:25.876 --> 00:37:27.345
And so people are
publicly committed

00:37:27.345 --> 00:37:31.349 align:start size:94% position:6%
to the current share,
before I entered the race.

00:37:31.349 --> 00:37:32.817 align:start size:94% position:6%
I mean, she had
committed, as I mentioned,

00:37:32.817 --> 00:37:34.619
to running only
for a third term,

00:37:34.619 --> 00:37:36.020
and she said she
was out after that.

00:37:36.020 --> 00:37:39.223 align:start size:97% position:3%
So it was kind of a
shocker to see her come out

00:37:39.223 --> 00:37:41.892
with this long list of
supporters, but whatever,

00:37:41.892 --> 00:37:43.127
I'm not deterred.

00:37:43.127 --> 00:37:44.528
I think it's gonna
go down to the wire.

00:37:44.528 --> 00:37:48.065 align:start size:97% position:3%
We have major donors
calling me, cold calling me,

00:37:48.065 --> 00:37:49.333
I've never spoken
to them before.

00:37:49.333 --> 00:37:51.602
Members of Congress,
United States Senators,

00:37:51.602 --> 00:37:53.904
and certainly some
members of the RNC,

00:37:53.904 --> 00:37:55.139 align:start size:84% position:16%
who called me before
I called them,

00:37:55.139 --> 00:37:56.307 align:start size:81% position:19%
saying, "Thank God
you're running,

00:37:56.307 --> 00:37:57.541 align:start size:84% position:16%
we really need a
change, I support you.

00:37:57.541 --> 00:37:58.709
How can I help you?"

00:37:58.709 --> 00:38:00.211
So it's been pretty encouraging.

00:38:00.211 --> 00:38:03.114
- Yeah, for the record, I'd
be happy to have Ronna on,

00:38:03.114 --> 00:38:05.516 align:start position:0%
and I would treat her the exact
same way I'm treating you,

00:38:05.516 --> 00:38:08.252 align:start size:84% position:16%
and ask the same type
of questions, and
all of that stuff.

00:38:08.252 --> 00:38:10.354
What do you think
the argument is?

00:38:10.354 --> 00:38:11.822
What is her main argument?

00:38:11.822 --> 00:38:15.126 align:start size:97% position:3%
I mean, because we all thought
this red wave was coming,

00:38:15.126 --> 00:38:17.662
and then it didn't come, and
they barely got the House.

00:38:17.662 --> 00:38:20.164 align:start size:81% position:19%
So it's like where
is the argument

00:38:20.164 --> 00:38:21.565 align:start size:84% position:16%
that you should stick
around for this,

00:38:21.565 --> 00:38:23.234
as you described
this fourth term,

00:38:23.234 --> 00:38:24.735
that was never supposed to be?

00:38:25.603 --> 00:38:26.437
- Yeah.

00:38:28.339 --> 00:38:30.207
- I'm really making
you be a lawyer now.

00:38:30.207 --> 00:38:32.109
- Yes, well, I am
an ethical person,

00:38:32.109 --> 00:38:34.478
so I will present this
being said by her team,

00:38:34.478 --> 00:38:36.547
which is, she did a great job.

00:38:37.682 --> 00:38:39.684 align:start size:84% position:16%
Four million more
Republicans turned out

00:38:39.684 --> 00:38:42.186
than in the prior cycle.

00:38:44.488 --> 00:38:46.223
We won back some seats
in different states,

00:38:46.223 --> 00:38:47.458
she's taken credit for that,

00:38:47.458 --> 00:38:49.293
she's taken credit for
the wins in Florida,

00:38:49.293 --> 00:38:50.761
New York, and California.

00:38:50.761 --> 00:38:53.564 align:start size:97% position:3%
I, you know, I think everyone
who lives in those states

00:38:53.564 --> 00:38:55.366 align:start size:84% position:16%
can make up their
own mind about that.

00:38:55.366 --> 00:38:57.702 align:start size:88% position:13%
I will leave it at that,
trying to be polite.

00:38:57.702 --> 00:39:00.004 align:start size:97% position:3%
People like her, personally,
and I don't dislike her.

00:39:00.004 --> 00:39:02.340
I'm not running against her
because of her personality.

00:39:02.340 --> 00:39:05.142
And you know, quote unquote,
I did a lot for your state.

00:39:05.142 --> 00:39:06.277
That's really what
I'm hearing back.

00:39:06.277 --> 00:39:08.045 align:start size:88% position:13%
I transferred X
dollars, to your state.

00:39:08.045 --> 00:39:10.514
I've offered to pay this,
I offered to pay that.

00:39:10.514 --> 00:39:13.250
Some of it is transactional,
some of it is relationship,

00:39:13.250 --> 00:39:15.086
and some of it is
so-called loyalty.

00:39:15.086 --> 00:39:17.221
Some of it is, I
asked you first.

00:39:17.221 --> 00:39:19.724
I mean, then one argument is,

00:39:19.724 --> 00:39:22.293
we shouldn't change horses
in the middle of the race.

00:39:22.293 --> 00:39:23.527
I mean, what's the race here?

00:39:23.527 --> 00:39:25.162 align:start size:88% position:13%
Like racing off a cliff,
as far as I can tell,

00:39:25.162 --> 00:39:26.897 align:start size:84% position:16%
we should definitely
change horses.

00:39:28.065 --> 00:39:30.067
Sailing analogy
is, I'm a sailor,

00:39:30.067 --> 00:39:32.236
is, you know, if you're
heading for the rocks,

00:39:32.236 --> 00:39:33.771 align:start size:84% position:16%
you should definitely
correct course.

00:39:33.771 --> 00:39:35.072
Maybe you need to reef the sails

00:39:35.072 --> 00:39:36.307
if there's a storm happening,

00:39:36.307 --> 00:39:38.676
and you need to like
definitely, you know,

00:39:38.676 --> 00:39:41.879 align:start size:97% position:3%
pull in some of those sail,
so you don't, you know, crash.

00:39:41.879 --> 00:39:42.813
And I think that's, you know,

00:39:42.813 --> 00:39:44.048
so that's the counter argument.

00:39:44.048 --> 00:39:45.282 align:start size:84% position:16%
But you asked me to
say what she's saying,

00:39:45.282 --> 00:39:48.686
"Yeah, steady, I've
done a great job,

00:39:48.686 --> 00:39:50.421
we should not change the course.

00:39:50.421 --> 00:39:54.425
We're already planning for
this convention and you know,

00:39:54.425 --> 00:39:57.027
we're doing great,"
that's the message.

00:39:58.229 --> 00:40:00.364
- Do you actually
think that the DNC

00:40:00.364 --> 00:40:03.534 align:start size:84% position:16%
is not as screwed
up and all the stuff

00:40:03.534 --> 00:40:05.102 align:start size:88% position:13%
that you just expressed
frustration with,

00:40:05.102 --> 00:40:06.837 align:start size:84% position:16%
and how deals get done
and all that stuff,

00:40:06.837 --> 00:40:09.240
that the DNC is not as screwy,

00:40:09.240 --> 00:40:11.475 align:start size:88% position:13%
or are they just better
at the screwiness?

00:40:11.475 --> 00:40:13.978
You know, like if you were
to look at the FTX situation,

00:40:13.978 --> 00:40:16.280
it's like they got all
the money, basically,

00:40:16.280 --> 00:40:18.082
I know he also gave
some to Republicans,

00:40:18.082 --> 00:40:19.784 align:start size:84% position:16%
but he was the second,
Sam Bankman-Fried,

00:40:19.784 --> 00:40:22.253
was second largest
donor to Democrats.

00:40:22.253 --> 00:40:24.989
And then of course two
days after the election,

00:40:24.989 --> 00:40:26.724
it all comes crashing down,

00:40:26.724 --> 00:40:30.327
meaning that they're
just somehow better
at the public show,

00:40:30.327 --> 00:40:31.695
and then we just move on,

00:40:31.695 --> 00:40:33.497
and you forget about
all the corruption,

00:40:33.497 --> 00:40:35.166
something like that?

00:40:35.166 --> 00:40:37.802
- So the Democrats
are definitely not
as screwy as we are,

00:40:37.802 --> 00:40:39.270
and I think they're not
screwy at all, actually,

00:40:39.270 --> 00:40:40.571
in the Democratic Party.

00:40:40.571 --> 00:40:43.040
They raise money in a
very disciplined way.

00:40:43.040 --> 00:40:45.743 align:start size:88% position:13%
And you know, they do
have their little tiffs,

00:40:45.743 --> 00:40:49.380
personality tiffs between
the squad and Nancy Pelosi.

00:40:49.380 --> 00:40:51.348 align:start size:94% position:6%
But overall I think
they're very disciplined.

00:40:51.348 --> 00:40:54.285
And now for the first couple
of years I was at the RNC,

00:40:54.285 --> 00:40:55.853
we would be cheering, "Rah rah,"

00:40:55.853 --> 00:40:58.055 align:start size:84% position:16%
because we raised more
money into the RNC

00:40:58.055 --> 00:41:01.425 align:start size:84% position:16%
than the DNC raises
into the DNC.

00:41:01.425 --> 00:41:02.960
And they're quietly laughing,

00:41:02.960 --> 00:41:05.996 align:start size:97% position:3%
because they raised 10 times
as much money as we do,

00:41:05.996 --> 00:41:09.500
but they raise it into
the NAACP, and the ACLU,

00:41:09.500 --> 00:41:11.402 align:start size:88% position:13%
and the Michigan Welfare
Rights Association,

00:41:11.402 --> 00:41:13.737
and La Raza, and
La Familia Vota.

00:41:13.737 --> 00:41:15.406 align:start size:88% position:13%
Those five organizations
are organizations

00:41:15.406 --> 00:41:17.174
that I'm up against
in court right now

00:41:17.174 --> 00:41:19.276
for election litigation.

00:41:19.276 --> 00:41:20.444
That's not the DNC.

00:41:20.444 --> 00:41:21.879 align:start size:84% position:16%
They have dark
money organizations

00:41:21.879 --> 00:41:23.581
that influence our elections,

00:41:23.581 --> 00:41:27.084
and help pick right
wing candidates in
moderate districts.

00:41:27.084 --> 00:41:29.687
And so that we just
like, we are cooked.

00:41:29.687 --> 00:41:31.288 align:start size:84% position:16%
We're thinking,
"Great, we selected

00:41:31.288 --> 00:41:33.591 align:start size:88% position:13%
this great conservative
candidate,"

00:41:33.591 --> 00:41:36.327
that candidate can't
win in that state.

00:41:36.327 --> 00:41:39.230
And so we're just like not
even talking about that.

00:41:39.230 --> 00:41:43.400
So their monetary ecosystem
is far superior to ours,

00:41:43.400 --> 00:41:45.236 align:start size:97% position:3%
and they're very
disciplined, and we are not,

00:41:45.236 --> 00:41:46.871
and we need to get smart.

00:41:46.871 --> 00:41:48.506
- All right, gimme
your final pitch,

00:41:48.506 --> 00:41:51.775
and where do you wanna send
people to find out more?

00:41:53.377 --> 00:41:56.647 align:start size:84% position:16%
- @PNJABAN on
Twitter is my handle.

00:41:56.647 --> 00:42:00.818 align:start size:94% position:6%
And then for my campaign,
dhillonforrnc.com,

00:42:00.818 --> 00:42:04.655
D-H-I-L-L-O-N-F-O-R-R-N-C.com

00:42:04.655 --> 00:42:06.190
And you know, please,

00:42:06.190 --> 00:42:08.959 align:start size:84% position:16%
if you are interested
in supporting us,

00:42:08.959 --> 00:42:11.195
reach out politely
to the three members

00:42:11.195 --> 00:42:13.931 align:start size:97% position:3%
of the RNC in your district,
do not call them names.

00:42:13.931 --> 00:42:16.367 align:start size:97% position:3%
Some of them are very
shocked to hear from voters.

00:42:16.367 --> 00:42:21.305
And importantly, if you're
a precinct committee man,

00:42:21.305 --> 00:42:22.540
or a state party delegate,

00:42:22.540 --> 00:42:24.074
and you play a role
in a state party,

00:42:24.074 --> 00:42:26.911
call for a vote of who should
be the next leader of the RNC.

00:42:26.911 --> 00:42:28.646
That happened in
Texas and Arizona,

00:42:28.646 --> 00:42:30.347 align:start size:81% position:19%
and a standing
vote in Tennessee,

00:42:30.347 --> 00:42:32.850 align:start size:84% position:16%
and they all support
overwhelmingly,

00:42:32.850 --> 00:42:35.286 align:start size:97% position:3%
and so that's been happening
all over the country.

00:42:35.286 --> 00:42:37.922
- Harmeet, I obviously
wish you a lot of luck.

00:42:37.922 --> 00:42:42.893 align:start size:97% position:3%
I also wish you merry
Christmas, and we shall see.

00:42:42.893 --> 00:42:45.930
- Absolutely, thanks
for having me, Dave.

00:42:45.930 --> 00:42:47.164
- My pleasure.

